window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 9, "domain": "assessment", "question": "You have twenty minutes and want this visit to be both safe and confidential for her. Before writing any prescription or ordering any test, which approach to the encounter is most appropriate for a young person who has presented alone with a request of this kind?", "options": {"A": "Explain confidentiality and its limits, then complete a HEEADSSS interview", "B": "Telephone her mother for permission before discussing contraception with her", "C": "Prescribe the contraceptive and book a return visit in three months", "D": "Refer her to a youth clinic because she is under sixteen years"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain confidentiality and its limits, then complete a HEEADSSS interview): stating what stays private and what must be reported before the interview begins is what makes honest answers possible, and the psychosocial screen surfaces the school absences, safety and mood issues that a contraception request can conceal. Contacting a parent without permission breaches confidentiality and ends the therapeutic relationship. Prescribing without the psychosocial assessment treats the request and misses the person. Age alone is not a barrier where the mature minor doctrine applies.", "case_id": "C003", "case_text": "A 15-year-old arrives alone at your clinic asking for contraception and says her mother must not find out. She is well, has no chronic illness, and takes no medications.\nShe answers questions readily, describes a relationship with a 16-year-old classmate, and reports missing several school days recently.\nBlood pressure is 112/70 mmHg and her body mass index sits at the 40th percentile for age. Your province applies the mature minor doctrine and sets no fixed minimum age for consent to treatment.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 10, "domain": "assessment", "question": "She describes correctly how the pill works, what to do about a missed dose, the risk of blood clots, and the option of an intrauterine device. She says she understands she could become pregnant without it. Which conclusion about consent is best supported here?", "options": {"A": "She cannot consent because provincial law requires a parent under sixteen", "B": "A capacity assessment by a psychiatrist is needed before prescribing safely", "C": "She meets the mature minor test and may consent to this treatment", "D": "Her consent is valid only if a parent countersigns the clinic record"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She meets the mature minor test and may consent to this treatment): she has demonstrated understanding of the nature of the treatment and appreciation of the consequences of accepting or refusing, which is exactly what the doctrine requires. The province in question sets no fixed age threshold, so a parental permission rule does not apply here. Specialist assessment is reserved for genuinely uncertain capacity, not for youth alone. A countersignature adds nothing legally and would breach her confidentiality.", "case_id": "C003", "case_text": "A 15-year-old arrives alone at your clinic asking for contraception and says her mother must not find out. She is well, has no chronic illness, and takes no medications.\nShe answers questions readily, describes a relationship with a 16-year-old classmate, and reports missing several school days recently.\nBlood pressure is 112/70 mmHg and her body mass index sits at the 40th percentile for age. Your province applies the mature minor doctrine and sets no fixed minimum age for consent to treatment.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 11, "domain": "assessment", "question": "During the psychosocial screen she discloses that she has been sleeping at different friends' homes for a month after conflict at home, and often does not eat until supper. She declines any contact with her parents. Which action should take priority alongside her contraception request?", "options": {"A": "Order a full nutritional blood panel including ferritin and vitamin D", "B": "Assess her immediate safety and connect her to youth housing supports", "C": "Document unstable housing and review the situation in three months", "D": "Notify her school so that attendance supports can be arranged"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess her immediate safety and connect her to youth housing supports): unstable housing and food insecurity are social determinants that predict outcomes more strongly than most clinical variables, and where a young person is sleeping tonight is both a safety question and an actionable one. Bloodwork does not change what happens to her this week. Documenting and waiting three months accepts a season of preventable risk. Contacting her school discloses personal information she has not agreed to share.", "case_id": "C003", "case_text": "A 15-year-old arrives alone at your clinic asking for contraception and says her mother must not find out. She is well, has no chronic illness, and takes no medications.\nShe answers questions readily, describes a relationship with a 16-year-old classmate, and reports missing several school days recently.\nBlood pressure is 112/70 mmHg and her body mass index sits at the 40th percentile for age. Your province applies the mature minor doctrine and sets no fixed minimum age for consent to treatment.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 12, "domain": "assessment", "question": "She agrees to a follow up in six weeks. You want that visit to evaluate whether your plan worked rather than simply repeat it. Which single measure at follow up would give you the most useful evidence about the effectiveness of this encounter?", "options": {"A": "Whether her blood pressure has remained within the normal range", "B": "Whether the contraceptive prescription was dispensed by the pharmacy", "C": "Whether her body mass index percentile has changed since this visit", "D": "Whether she now has stable housing and reliable access to food"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Whether she now has stable housing and reliable access to food): the intervention with the largest effect on her health was the referral for housing and food, so evaluation has to test whether that referral actually produced a change. A stable blood pressure was never the problem being treated. Pharmacy dispensing records tell you a prescription moved, not whether her circumstances improved. A percentile shift over six weeks is too small and too slow to inform anything.", "case_id": "C003", "case_text": "A 15-year-old arrives alone at your clinic asking for contraception and says her mother must not find out. She is well, has no chronic illness, and takes no medications.\nShe answers questions readily, describes a relationship with a 16-year-old classmate, and reports missing several school days recently.\nBlood pressure is 112/70 mmHg and her body mass index sits at the 40th percentile for age. Your province applies the mature minor doctrine and sets no fixed minimum age for consent to treatment.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 13, "domain": "assessment", "question": "Your clinic is attached to a community health centre with laboratory access and the regional pediatric hospital is twenty minutes away. Which action is the priority for this infant right now, before any further history about the pregnancy or family illness is obtained?", "options": {"A": "Give oral acetaminophen and reassess the temperature in one hour", "B": "Collect a urine sample and start oral antibiotics before arranging any transfer", "C": "Advise increased feeding and arrange review the following morning", "D": "Arrange immediate transfer for a full septic workup and parenteral antibiotics"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Arrange immediate transfer for a full septic workup and parenteral antibiotics): any rectal temperature of 38.0 degrees Celsius or higher in an infant under three months is emergent, and sleepiness with poor feeding raises the concern further. Antipyretics treat a number and delay the workup that decides outcome. A urine sample alone misses meningitis and bacteremia, and oral antibiotics are inadequate for possible serious bacterial infection at this age. Next morning review accepts an overnight deterioration risk that this age group cannot tolerate.", "case_id": "C004", "case_text": "A 7-week-old infant is brought to your clinic by both parents because she has fed poorly since yesterday and feels warm. She was born at 39 weeks after an uncomplicated delivery and her newborn screening was normal.\nToday she is sleepy but rousable and has had three wet diapers in twenty four hours. Her rectal temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius.\nHeart rate is 178 beats per minute and respiratory rate is 58 breaths per minute. The anterior fontanelle is soft and flat.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 14, "domain": "assessment", "question": "The parents point out that the fontanelle is soft, there is no rash, and the baby settles when held. They ask whether these reassuring findings mean serious infection is unlikely. Which statement best reflects how these findings should be weighted in your reasoning?", "options": {"A": "Young infants show few localizing signs, so absent signs cannot exclude sepsis", "B": "A soft fontanelle reliably excludes meningitis in infants under three months", "C": "Settling when held indicates a normal neurological examination in this age group", "D": "Absence of rash makes bacterial infection substantially less likely than viral illness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Young infants show few localizing signs, so absent signs cannot exclude sepsis): infants of this age present with non specific feeding change, temperature instability and lethargy rather than focal findings, so the absence of classic signs carries almost no negative predictive value. A soft fontanelle is common early in meningitis and cannot rule it out. Consolability is reassuring in an older child but does not constitute a neurological assessment here. Bacterial and viral illness overlap completely in appearance at seven weeks.", "case_id": "C004", "case_text": "A 7-week-old infant is brought to your clinic by both parents because she has fed poorly since yesterday and feels warm. She was born at 39 weeks after an uncomplicated delivery and her newborn screening was normal.\nToday she is sleepy but rousable and has had three wet diapers in twenty four hours. Her rectal temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius.\nHeart rate is 178 beats per minute and respiratory rate is 58 breaths per minute. The anterior fontanelle is soft and flat.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 15, "domain": "assessment", "question": "Transport has been called and will arrive in fifteen minutes. The infant remains sleepy, capillary refill is three seconds, and she has taken only ten millilitres of formula since morning. Which intervention should you prioritise during the wait for the transport team?", "options": {"A": "Perform a lumbar puncture so results are ready on arrival", "B": "Weigh the infant and plot the value on a growth chart", "C": "Establish vascular access and begin an isotonic fluid bolus", "D": "Attempt a further formula feed by bottle while awaiting transport"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Establish vascular access and begin an isotonic fluid bolus): delayed capillary refill with tachycardia and reduced intake indicates impaired perfusion, and restoring circulating volume is the intervention that changes physiology within minutes. A lumbar puncture in an unstable infant is unsafe and belongs to the receiving team. Plotting growth is useful surveillance but does nothing for perfusion now. Oral feeding is unreliable in a sleepy infant and carries an aspiration risk.", "case_id": "C004", "case_text": "A 7-week-old infant is brought to your clinic by both parents because she has fed poorly since yesterday and feels warm. She was born at 39 weeks after an uncomplicated delivery and her newborn screening was normal.\nToday she is sleepy but rousable and has had three wet diapers in twenty four hours. Her rectal temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius.\nHeart rate is 178 beats per minute and respiratory rate is 58 breaths per minute. The anterior fontanelle is soft and flat.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 16, "domain": "assessment", "question": "Two weeks after discharge with a treated urinary tract infection, the infant returns for review. She is feeding well and afebrile. Her parents are anxious and ask what should happen now. Which follow up action is most appropriate to prioritise at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Repeat blood cultures and inflammatory markers to confirm the infection cleared", "B": "Plot weight on the growth chart and confirm renal imaging is arranged", "C": "Begin daily prophylactic antibiotics until the second birthday is reached", "D": "Delay the next immunizations by one month because of the recent infection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Plot weight on the growth chart and confirm renal imaging is arranged): recovery is judged by feeding and weight trajectory, and a febrile urinary tract infection in a young infant requires the imaging pathway to be completed rather than assumed. Repeat cultures in a well afebrile infant add cost without changing management. Routine long term prophylaxis is not supported after a first uncomplicated episode. A resolved infection is not a contraindication to immunization on the provincial schedule.", "case_id": "C004", "case_text": "A 7-week-old infant is brought to your clinic by both parents because she has fed poorly since yesterday and feels warm. She was born at 39 weeks after an uncomplicated delivery and her newborn screening was normal.\nToday she is sleepy but rousable and has had three wet diapers in twenty four hours. Her rectal temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius.\nHeart rate is 178 beats per minute and respiratory rate is 58 breaths per minute. The anterior fontanelle is soft and flat.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 21, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 47-year-old books a ten minute appointment for a blood pressure recheck. As you finish and reach for the door handle, she says quietly that she has been feeling unsafe at home. You are already running twenty minutes behind schedule. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Book a longer appointment next week to explore the disclosure properly", "B": "Give her a printed list of community shelters and end the visit", "C": "Ask the clinic social worker to telephone her later this afternoon", "D": "Sit back down and assess her immediate safety before she leaves"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sit back down and assess her immediate safety before she leaves): a doorknob disclosure of feeling unsafe is an acuity question, and whether she is safe to go home tonight cannot be deferred to a future appointment. Rebooking signals that the concern matters less than the schedule, and she may not return. A printed list without assessment neither establishes risk nor makes a plan. A telephone call later leaves the interval between now and then completely unassessed."}, {"id": 28, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 66-year-old woman is incapable of consenting to a feeding tube after a large stroke. Her estranged husband, her adult son, and her sister all attend and disagree. She named no attorney for personal care and no guardian has been appointed. Who decides?", "options": {"A": "The adult son, because he has been most involved in her care", "B": "The clinical team, because the family cannot reach an agreement", "C": "The spouse, unless separation meets the statutory test excluding him", "D": "The sister, because she is the only relative without a conflict"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The spouse, unless separation meets the statutory test excluding him): substitute decision making follows a ranked hierarchy in which spouse or partner outranks child and sibling, and estrangement removes a spouse only if the jurisdiction's definition of separation is actually met. Involvement in care is not a ranking criterion and does not promote a child above a spouse. Disagreement among family does not transfer authority to the clinical team. Absence of conflict is not a legal basis for selecting a substitute."}, {"id": 29, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A community pharmacist telephones asking for the indication behind a new prescription so she can counsel your client appropriately. The client has not been asked about this specific disclosure. Which action best reflects Canadian health privacy expectations in this particular situation?", "options": {"A": "Refuse, since express written consent is required for every disclosure", "B": "Disclose the full chart so the pharmacist can counsel most safely", "C": "Share the indication, since the pharmacist is within the circle of care", "D": "Ask the pharmacist to obtain written authorization from the client directly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Share the indication, since the pharmacist is within the circle of care): provincial privacy statutes generally permit implied consent for sharing information among clinicians providing that person's care, and a dispensing pharmacist counselling on the same prescription sits inside that circle. Requiring written consent for every internal exchange would make coordinated care impossible. Sending the whole chart exceeds what is needed and breaches the limiting principle. Pushing the paperwork onto the pharmacist misstates where the obligation actually sits."}, {"id": 31, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 40-year-old reports a penicillin allergy. When you ask what happened, she describes nausea and loose stools as a child after amoxicillin, with no rash, swelling or breathing difficulty. She now needs treatment for a dental abscess. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe a fluoroquinolone to avoid any risk of cross reactivity", "B": "Refer to an allergist and delay antibiotics until testing is complete", "C": "Record the allergy as confirmed and prescribe clindamycin for the abscess", "D": "Reclassify it as intolerance and prescribe amoxicillin with clear counselling"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reclassify it as intolerance and prescribe amoxicillin with clear counselling): gastrointestinal upset without rash, angioedema or respiratory symptoms is a known side effect rather than an immune mediated reaction, and leaving the label unchallenged narrows her options for life. Broad spectrum alternatives such as fluoroquinolones carry greater harm and resistance cost. Delaying treatment of an abscess for allergy testing is disproportionate to the history described. Recording an unverified label as confirmed entrenches the error in her record."}, {"id": 46, "domain": "assessment", "question": "You want to add routine social determinant screening to every periodic health review in your practice. A colleague argues this wastes visit time, since a nurse practitioner cannot change anyone's income or housing. Which argument best supports incorporating this screening into your assessments?", "options": {"A": "Findings change management directly, including what you prescribe and where you refer", "B": "Screening documents inequity for research purposes even when nothing can change", "C": "Regulatory colleges now require social screening at every periodic health review", "D": "Clients expect these questions and rate their satisfaction higher afterwards"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Findings change management directly, including what you prescribe and where you refer): knowing that a person cannot afford a medication, has no refrigeration, or has no fixed address changes the prescription, the follow up interval and the referral, which is the definition of an assessment that alters management. Documenting inequity without acting is surveillance rather than care. No college mandates a specific screening item at every review. Satisfaction is a weak justification compared with a change in clinical decisions."}, {"id": 52, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 31-year-old with type 2 diabetes and hypertension tells you she hopes to conceive within the year. She takes metformin, ramipril and atorvastatin, and her glycated hemoglobin is 8.4 percent. Which action is the highest priority at this preconception visit today?", "options": {"A": "Begin folic acid at 0.4 milligrams daily and review her in six months", "B": "Replace the teratogenic medications and start high dose folic acid now", "C": "Refer to obstetrics and defer all medication decisions to that service", "D": "Delay any change until a pregnancy test confirms that she has conceived"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Replace the teratogenic medications and start high dose folic acid now): angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors and statins are contraindicated in pregnancy and must be switched before conception rather than after a positive test, and diabetes places her in the higher folic acid dose group. The standard 0.4 milligram dose is insufficient for a woman with diabetes. Referral does not remove the primary care responsibility to act on a teratogen today. Waiting for a positive test means exposure occurs during organogenesis."}, {"id": 53, "domain": "assessment", "question": "An 81-year-old man from a long term care home is brought in after two days of reduced oral intake. Staff report he has been more confused than usual. His vital signs on arrival are shown. What is your interpretation and immediate priority?", "options": {"A": "Dehydration only, so oral rehydration and observation in the clinic suffices", "B": "Normal ageing changes, so review him again in twenty four hours", "C": "Possible sepsis, so begin resuscitation and arrange urgent hospital transfer", "D": "Likely urinary infection, so start oral antibiotics and send him back"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Possible sepsis, so begin resuscitation and arrange urgent hospital transfer): tachycardia, tachypnoea, hypotension and new confusion together indicate an emergent presentation, and older adults frequently mount no fever or even become hypothermic. Attributing the picture to dehydration alone ignores the perfusion failure the numbers describe. None of these findings represent normal ageing. Committing to a urinary source and oral antibiotics narrows the differential before it has been assessed and delays definitive care.", "image": "question_images/q_53.png"}, {"id": 58, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 44-year-old labourer had the imaging shown ordered by a walk in clinic for six weeks of mechanical low back pain without red flags. He now asks you what it means and whether he needs surgery. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Refer him to spinal surgery to review the disc changes described", "B": "Repeat the imaging in six months to track the degenerative changes", "C": "Order computed tomography to better characterise the facet joint findings", "D": "Explain these findings are common and incidental, and continue active management"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain these findings are common and incidental, and continue active management): disc desiccation and facet arthropathy are present in most asymptomatic adults of this age, and imaging for uncomplicated back pain without red flags is exactly the practice Choosing Wisely Canada advises against because it drives cascades like this one. Surgical referral for incidental degenerative change exposes him to intervention without indication. Repeat imaging tracks findings that do not correlate with symptoms. Further imaging compounds the original error.", "image": "question_images/q_58.png"}, {"id": 60, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 29-year-old attends for an unrelated concern. You are taking a pharmacological history and want to ask about cannabis, alcohol and any medications she may have obtained from others. Which approach is most likely to produce an accurate account from her today?", "options": {"A": "Ask everyone the same normalising questions as part of routine history", "B": "Ask only when the presenting concern suggests substance use is relevant", "C": "Ask the questions after the physical examination, when rapport is strongest", "D": "Ask her partner separately to corroborate what she reports to you"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask everyone the same normalising questions as part of routine history): asking the same questions of everyone removes the signal that you suspect this person in particular, and normalising language makes disclosure of borrowed medications, cannabis and alcohol far more likely. Asking only when you suspect something guarantees you miss the people who do not fit your expectation. Deferring to the end of the visit frequently means the questions are never asked. Seeking corroboration from a partner without consent breaches privacy."}, {"id": 61, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A new client transfers to your practice with a thick paper chart and reports fatigue. You have twenty minutes and cannot read everything in it. Which strategy for using the previous record is most likely to change what you actually do at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Read the record chronologically from the earliest entry available forward", "B": "Read only the most recent progress note written by her previous clinician", "C": "Set the previous record aside and take a completely fresh independent history today", "D": "Review prior investigations and diagnoses relevant to fatigue before examining her"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Review prior investigations and diagnoses relevant to fatigue before examining her): targeted retrieval of what bears on the presenting concern prevents you from repeating tests already done and reveals patterns the person may not recall. Reading chronologically from the beginning consumes the visit and rarely reaches the relevant material. A single recent note gives a snapshot without the trend that makes the data meaningful. Ignoring the record entirely discards years of information and guarantees duplicate testing."}, {"id": 63, "domain": "assessment", "question": "You call the next client from the waiting room. He walks slowly, pauses twice, speaks in three word phrases and has grey lips. He has not yet told you why he came. Which of these should determine what you do in the next ten seconds?", "options": {"A": "The reason for the encounter recorded by the receptionist at booking", "B": "The airway, breathing, circulation and distress you can already observe", "C": "The last documented problem list entry in his electronic health record", "D": "The results of his most recent bloodwork available in the system"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The airway, breathing, circulation and distress you can already observe): the initial assessment is a rapid physiological survey that happens before any history and decides whether this encounter is an interview or a resuscitation. A booking reason describes what he intended to discuss, not his current stability. The problem list is historical and cannot describe the state he is in as he walks toward you. Previous bloodwork tells you nothing about his work of breathing right now."}, {"id": 95, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 20-month-old girl used about ten words at her eighteen month visit and now uses none. She previously waved and pointed and has stopped doing both. Her parents attribute the change to the arrival of a new baby sister. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Reassure them that regression is common after the birth of a sibling", "B": "Refer urgently for developmental assessment, since skill loss is never normal", "C": "Screen for iron deficiency and recheck her language again in about three months", "D": "Advise more one to one play and review at the two year well child visit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer urgently for developmental assessment, since skill loss is never normal): loss of previously acquired language and social gestures is a red flag that requires prompt evaluation for autism spectrum disorder, hearing loss, seizure disorder and neurodegenerative and metabolic conditions. Behavioural regression after a new sibling may affect toileting or sleep, but it does not erase established communication milestones. Iron studies are reasonable in parallel and never sufficient on their own. Watchful waiting with more play delays diagnosis of a condition where the earliest intervention produces the largest functional gain."}, {"id": 97, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 3-year-old newly arrived from another country has a paper record showing two doses of a diphtheria containing vaccine and one measles dose given at nine months of age. His parents want him ready to start preschool. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Build a catch up schedule using the Canadian Immunization Guide and this record", "B": "Restart the entire schedule from the beginning because the record is foreign", "C": "Order serology for every vaccine preventable disease before giving any doses", "D": "Accept the record as complete and give only the routine preschool boosters"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Build a catch up schedule using the Canadian Immunization Guide and this record): written records from other countries are accepted as valid, and the Guide provides age appropriate catch up intervals for children whose immunization is incomplete or started early. A measles dose given at nine months does not count toward the routine series in Canada and must be repeated. Restarting everything exposes the child to unnecessary injections and delays protection. Blanket serology is costly, poorly standardized for several antigens, and unnecessary when a documented record exists, and treating the record as complete leaves him underimmunized entering group care."}, {"id": 100, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 2-year-old girl is brought because her parents think she is small. Her weight has tracked steadily along the same percentile band throughout, she is developing normally, and she eats well. Review the length chart provided. Which interpretation best directs your next step?", "options": {"A": "Length is normal because she has grown at every measurement point recorded", "B": "Short stature is confirmed and growth hormone testing should be arranged now", "C": "Length has drifted downward across bands, so further evaluation is warranted", "D": "Parental height alone explains the pattern, so no further assessment is needed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Length has drifted downward across bands, so further evaluation is warranted): starting near the median and falling progressively below it while weight holds its band is linear growth failure, which points toward endocrine, skeletal, genetic or chronic disease causes rather than simple undernutrition. Where undernutrition is the driver, weight usually falters before length. Continued absolute growth does not exclude a falling velocity, which is precisely what percentile crossing detects. Familial short stature is a diagnosis of exclusion that requires measured parental heights and a mid parental target, and growth hormone testing is not a first line investigation.", "image": "question_images/q_100.png"}, {"id": 107, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 22-year-old who became sexually active at nineteen asks for a Pap test because a friend was told to have one. She has no symptoms and is in a monogamous relationship. Her provincial programme begins cervical screening at twenty five. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Perform the test today, since patient request is sufficient clinical justification", "B": "Perform the test because early sexual debut lowers the screening start age", "C": "Explain the screening schedule and the harms of testing before that age", "D": "Tell her that cervical screening has been discontinued across Canada"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the screening schedule and the harms of testing before that age): transient human papillomavirus infection is very common in the early twenties and clears spontaneously, so screening before the programme start age mostly detects changes that would resolve, leading to colposcopy, excisional treatment and later obstetric risk without reducing cancer deaths. Explaining that reasoning respects her autonomy while declining an intervention that would harm her. Age of first intercourse does not shift the start age in any Canadian provincial or territorial programme. Screening remains active in every province, so telling her it has ended is simply false."}, {"id": 135, "domain": "assessment", "question": "Before deciding whether radiography is indicated, you plan to apply the Ottawa Ankle Rules to his malleolar zone pain. Which specific palpation sites and which functional test together define that rule, and therefore determine whether an ankle radiograph series is warranted today?", "options": {"A": "Tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament, plus painful weight bearing today", "B": "Tenderness at the base of the fifth metatarsal, plus a limping gait", "C": "Tenderness over the Achilles insertion, plus inability to stand on tiptoe", "D": "Tenderness at either malleolar posterior edge or tip, plus four step inability"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tenderness at either malleolar posterior edge or tip, plus four step inability): the malleolar component requires bone tenderness along the posterior edge or tip of either malleolus, or inability to bear weight for four steps both immediately after injury and in the assessment setting. Ligament tenderness is soft tissue and is deliberately excluded from the rule. The fifth metatarsal base belongs to the midfoot component for foot radiography. Achilles findings sit outside both Ottawa components entirely.", "case_id": "C012", "case_text": "A 34-year-old carpenter inverted his right ankle stepping off a curb ninety\nminutes ago. He walked into your clinic with a limp. He reports pain on the\nouter side of the ankle with swelling. He has no other injuries, takes no\nmedications, and has never fractured this ankle. He is afebrile with normal\nvital signs and removed his own work boot without assistance.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 136, "domain": "assessment", "question": "Examination reveals maximal tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament with no bony tenderness at either malleolus, no midfoot tenderness, and he takes eight painful but unaided steps in your room. What is the correct interpretation of the Ottawa Ankle Rules here, and what is the appropriate next step in his care today?", "options": {"A": "Rule positive, so order ankle and foot radiographs before any bracing", "B": "Rule negative, so treat as a sprain with protected weight bearing", "C": "Rule indeterminate, so reassess in seventy two hours before imaging decisions", "D": "Rule not applicable, because pain limits an accurate weight bearing test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rule negative, so treat as a sprain with protected weight bearing): no bone tenderness at either malleolus and the ability to take four or more steps make the malleolar component negative, and absent midfoot tenderness makes the foot component negative. The rules are highly sensitive by design, so a negative result reliably excludes clinically significant fracture. Painful but successful ambulation still counts as bearing weight, so the rule remains applicable. Deferring the decision three days leaves him without a plan.", "case_id": "C012", "case_text": "A 34-year-old carpenter inverted his right ankle stepping off a curb ninety\nminutes ago. He walked into your clinic with a limp. He reports pain on the\nouter side of the ankle with swelling. He has no other injuries, takes no\nmedications, and has never fractured this ankle. He is afebrile with normal\nvital signs and removed his own work boot without assistance.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 137, "domain": "assessment", "question": "He asks whether he should get an x-ray anyway, saying a friend with a similar injury received one. Which response best reflects both the evidence behind the Ottawa rules and Choosing Wisely Canada messaging about unnecessary imaging in acute ankle injury presenting to primary care?", "options": {"A": "The rules miss almost no fractures, so imaging now would change nothing", "B": "Imaging is reasonable since radiation from an ankle film is negligible", "C": "A radiograph is needed to document the injury for his employer records", "D": "Films are deferred only because clinic wait times exceed forty eight hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The rules miss almost no fractures, so imaging now would change nothing): the Ottawa rules were derived and validated to be near perfectly sensitive, accepting low specificity so that a negative result is safe. Choosing Wisely Canada targets exactly this scenario, since routine films after a negative rule add cost and false reassurance without altering treatment. Low radiation dose does not justify a test that cannot change management. Withholding the film is a clinical judgement, not a scheduling or paperwork issue.", "case_id": "C012", "case_text": "A 34-year-old carpenter inverted his right ankle stepping off a curb ninety\nminutes ago. He walked into your clinic with a limp. He reports pain on the\nouter side of the ankle with swelling. He has no other injuries, takes no\nmedications, and has never fractured this ankle. He is afebrile with normal\nvital signs and removed his own work boot without assistance.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 138, "domain": "assessment", "question": "You review him ten days later. Swelling has improved but he now reports point tenderness over the base of the fifth metatarsal that was absent initially, and he limps after two hours on his feet. What is the most appropriate action now?", "options": {"A": "Continue rehabilitation, since the Ottawa rules were negative at presentation", "B": "Order magnetic resonance imaging to look for occult ligamentous disruption", "C": "Order foot radiographs, since midfoot bone tenderness is now present", "D": "Refer urgently to orthopedics for suspected Lisfranc injury without imaging"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Order foot radiographs, since midfoot bone tenderness is now present): the Ottawa Foot Rule indicates radiography for midfoot zone pain with bone tenderness at the base of the fifth metatarsal or the navicular. A rule applied at one visit does not immunise later visits, and new bony tenderness is exactly the trigger it describes. Continuing rehabilitation ignores a finding that may represent a missed or stress fracture. Advanced imaging and specialist referral follow a plain film rather than replacing it.", "case_id": "C012", "case_text": "A 34-year-old carpenter inverted his right ankle stepping off a curb ninety\nminutes ago. He walked into your clinic with a limp. He reports pain on the\nouter side of the ankle with swelling. He has no other injuries, takes no\nmedications, and has never fractured this ankle. He is afebrile with normal\nvital signs and removed his own work boot without assistance.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 156, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 61-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has a rapidly spreading red area on his left calf. Pain is far worse than the appearance suggests, he is febrile at 39.2 degrees Celsius, tachycardic at 122, and you feel crepitus. Which action is the priority?", "options": {"A": "Mark the erythema border carefully and reassess him in one hour", "B": "Arrange immediate surgical assessment and start intravenous antibiotics now", "C": "Obtain a wound swab and start oral cephalexin with next day review", "D": "Order a lower limb ultrasound to exclude deep vein thrombosis first"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arrange immediate surgical assessment and start intravenous antibiotics now): pain out of proportion, crepitus, rapid spread and systemic toxicity describe necrotising fasciitis, a surgical emergency where survival depends on early debridement. Marking the border is a useful adjunct in cellulitis but wastes an hour when the diagnosis already demands the operating room. Oral antibiotics cannot penetrate necrotic fascia and next day review is far too late. Imaging for thrombosis pursues a different diagnosis and delays definitive treatment."}, {"id": 157, "domain": "assessment", "question": "An 80-year-old woman has a shallow ulcer over the medial malleolus with surrounding hemosiderin staining and edema. Her pedal pulses are not palpable through the swelling and she reports calf pain when walking one block. What should you do before selecting a dressing or a compression plan?", "options": {"A": "Apply multilayer compression immediately, since the appearance is clearly venous", "B": "Swab the ulcer base and start antibiotics before any compression is applied", "C": "Elevate the limb for one week and reassess the pedal pulses afterwards", "D": "Measure the ankle brachial index to establish arterial perfusion first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Measure the ankle brachial index to establish arterial perfusion first): the ulcer looks venous, but absent pulses and claudication raise the possibility of coexisting arterial disease, and compression applied to an ischemic limb causes tissue loss. Vascular assessment therefore precedes the treatment decision rather than following it. Swabbing a chronic ulcer without signs of infection yields colonising organisms and drives unnecessary antibiotics. A week of elevation delays both the diagnosis and the definitive management of an established wound."}, {"id": 167, "domain": "assessment", "question": "An 81-year-old woman reports six weeks of fatigue, palpitations and a five kilogram weight loss despite a good appetite. She feels warm and has a fine tremor. Her rhythm strip is shown. Which conclusion best integrates the strip with her clinical picture?", "options": {"A": "New atrial fibrillation here should prompt thyroid function testing as a cause", "B": "The strip shows sinus tachycardia, which is expected with anxiety and tremor", "C": "The rhythm is unrelated to her symptoms and needs no further evaluation", "D": "Second degree heart block explains her fatigue and requires pacemaker referral"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (New atrial fibrillation here should prompt thyroid function testing as a cause): the strip shows an irregularly irregular rhythm without discernible P waves, and thyrotoxicosis is a recognised precipitant that presents in older adults with weight loss, heat intolerance and tremor. Sinus tachycardia would show regular intervals with a P wave before every complex, which is not displayed. Dismissing a new arrhythmia in a symptomatic client ignores both stroke risk and a treatable underlying cause. Nothing on the strip shows progressive lengthening or dropped conduction.", "image": "question_images/q_167.png"}, {"id": 168, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 64-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes reports two hours of epigastric heaviness, nausea and sweating. Her chest wall is mildly tender. Blood pressure is 138/84 mmHg and saturation 97 percent. Her initial rhythm strip is shown. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Reassure and treat as musculoskeletal pain given the reproducible chest tenderness", "B": "Arrange emergency transfer with serial twelve lead tracings and troponin testing", "C": "Prescribe a proton pump inhibitor trial and review her in one week", "D": "Order an outpatient exercise stress test within the next seven days"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arrange emergency transfer with serial twelve lead tracings and troponin testing): women, older adults and people with diabetes often present with epigastric discomfort, nausea and diaphoresis rather than classic chest pain, and a single unremarkable tracing never excludes acute coronary syndrome. Reproducible chest wall tenderness lowers probability slightly but is present in a meaningful minority of confirmed infarcts. Empiric acid suppression treats a diagnosis of exclusion while an evolving infarct goes unmonitored. Stress testing during a possible acute presentation is unsafe and delays care by days.", "image": "question_images/q_168.png"}, {"id": 172, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 74-year-old male smoker presents with sudden severe back pain radiating to the left flank and a feeling of faintness. Blood pressure is 88/52 mmHg and heart rate 118. You palpate a pulsatile expansile mass above the umbilicus. What is the correct immediate action in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Arrange an urgent outpatient computed tomography angiogram within twenty four hours", "B": "Obtain a bedside urinalysis to exclude an obstructing renal calculus first", "C": "Give intravenous fluids to a normal blood pressure before any transfer", "D": "Activate emergency vascular transfer now without waiting for any imaging"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Activate emergency vascular transfer now without waiting for any imaging): a pulsatile expansile mass with back pain and hypotension is a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, and imaging happens only when the client is stable enough to tolerate the delay. This client is already shocked, so definitive surgical control is the only intervention that changes the outcome. Urinalysis pursues renal colic, which does not explain a pulsatile mass with hypotension. Aggressive fluid resuscitation to a normal pressure worsens bleeding, since permissive hypotension is preferred until the aorta is controlled."}, {"id": 174, "domain": "assessment", "question": "An 84-year-old nursing home resident had an unwitnessed fall. She is afebrile, her chest and abdomen are normal, and she has no urinary symptoms. A dipstick sent by staff shows nitrites and leukocytes, and culture grows Escherichia coli. What is the best course of action?", "options": {"A": "Treat with nitrofurantoin for five days and repeat the urine culture afterwards", "B": "Treat with a single dose of fosfomycin and monitor closely for delirium", "C": "Withhold antibiotics and investigate other causes of the fall thoroughly", "D": "Repeat the urine culture in one week and treat if the growth persists"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold antibiotics and investigate other causes of the fall thoroughly): Choosing Wisely Canada is explicit that asymptomatic bacteriuria should not be screened for or treated outside pregnancy and certain urologic procedures, and a positive culture in an older adult without urinary symptoms does not explain a fall. Treating produces resistance, Clostridioides difficile risk and adverse drug effects while the real cause goes unexamined. A single dose regimen is still unnecessary antibiotic exposure. Repeating the culture perpetuates the original testing error rather than correcting it."}, {"id": 176, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 26-year-old woman has six weeks of amenorrhea, left lower abdominal pain and light vaginal bleeding. She is hemodynamically stable. Her serum beta human chorionic gonadotropin is 3200 international units per litre. The ultrasound report is shown. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the beta human chorionic gonadotropin in forty eight hours at home", "B": "Reassure that an early intrauterine pregnancy is likely and review weekly", "C": "Treat as threatened miscarriage with rest and outpatient bleeding precautions", "D": "Refer same day to obstetrics and gynecology for suspected ectopic pregnancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer same day to obstetrics and gynecology for suspected ectopic pregnancy): an empty uterus with a separate adnexal structure and free pelvic fluid, at a hormone level above the discriminatory zone, describes an ectopic pregnancy until specialist assessment proves otherwise. Serial hormone testing has a role when imaging is genuinely indeterminate, but it is unsafe here because an adnexal mass and free fluid are already present. Reassurance about an early intrauterine pregnancy contradicts both the level and the imaging. Managing this as a miscarriage risks rupture and hemorrhage at home.", "image": "question_images/q_176.png"}, {"id": 182, "domain": "assessment", "question": "An 82-year-old woman with mild memory complaints is brought in because over two days she has become drowsy in the morning and agitated at night, with inattention that varies hour to hour. Which conclusion about this presentation is most accurate for this client?", "options": {"A": "This is expected progression of dementia and warrants cognitive testing later", "B": "This is delirium, a medical emergency requiring a search for causes", "C": "This is late life depression presenting with psychomotor and sleep disturbance", "D": "This is normal age related slowing worsened by an unfamiliar clinical environment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (This is delirium, a medical emergency requiring a search for causes): acute onset over days, a fluctuating course and impaired attention are the defining features, and delirium always signals an underlying medical problem such as infection, medication effect, urinary retention or a metabolic derangement. Dementia progresses over months to years with attention preserved early, so a two day change does not fit. Depression can cause slowing but not hour to hour fluctuation in attention. Normal ageing produces slower processing without impaired attention or a reversed sleep pattern."}, {"id": 208, "domain": "assessment", "question": "You suspect that something in the home is going wrong, but almost no information so far has come from the man himself. His son is becoming irritated by your questions and keeps checking the time on his phone. What is the most appropriate first step in this assessment?", "options": {"A": "Report the situation to police before speaking further with either man", "B": "Ask the son directly whether he has been hurting or neglecting his father", "C": "Document the bruising and arrange a home visit within the next month", "D": "Create a clinical reason to interview the man privately without his son"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Create a clinical reason to interview the man privately without his son): a companion who will not leave the room is itself an indicator, and no valid abuse assessment is possible while the suspected person is present. Confronting the son first can escalate danger for the older adult and closes off any later disclosure. Delaying a home visit by a month leaves a possibly unsafe situation unexamined. Contacting police before the man has spoken removes his voice from a decision he may have capacity to make.", "case_id": "C019", "case_text": "An 84-year-old man with moderate osteoarthritis and early Alzheimer disease lives with his son in Ontario.\nHe arrives for a routine visit noticeably thinner than at his last review, wearing soiled clothing, with bruises of different ages on both forearms.\nHis son answers every question for him and declines to leave the room. The community pharmacy reports that his medications have not been picked up for two months. He scores 21 on the MoCA.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 209, "domain": "assessment", "question": "The son states that his father has dementia, cannot make any decisions at all, and that the cognitive screening score proves it, so every decision should now be made by him. You need to respond accurately about what that score does establish. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "A score of 21 confirms incapacity for all health and financial decisions", "B": "The score signals impairment, but capacity is decision specific and assessed separately", "C": "The score is a capacity assessment only when it is performed by a physician", "D": "Dementia at any stage removes capacity to consent to health care decisions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The score signals impairment, but capacity is decision specific and assessed separately): a cognitive screen quantifies impairment and never determines capacity for any particular decision. Capacity turns on whether the person can understand the relevant information and appreciate the reasonably foreseeable consequences of deciding or not deciding. A dementia diagnosis lowers the probability of capacity for complex decisions but does not remove it, and capacity is presumed in Canada. The professional discipline of the administrator does not convert a screening score into a capacity finding.", "case_id": "C019", "case_text": "An 84-year-old man with moderate osteoarthritis and early Alzheimer disease lives with his son in Ontario.\nHe arrives for a routine visit noticeably thinner than at his last review, wearing soiled clothing, with bruises of different ages on both forearms.\nHis son answers every question for him and declines to leave the room. The community pharmacy reports that his medications have not been picked up for two months. He scores 21 on the MoCA.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 210, "domain": "assessment", "question": "Interviewed alone, he says his son takes his pension cheque and that the bruises come from being pulled roughly out of bed. He understands his situation, states clearly that he does not want police involved, and lives in his son's private home. What is your obligation?", "options": {"A": "Mandatory reporting applies in every province whenever elder abuse is suspected", "B": "Report to the long term care regulator, since neglect is suspected here", "C": "No blanket duty applies, so safety plan and support his capable choices", "D": "Report to adult protection services, which every province requires by statute"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No blanket duty applies, so safety plan and support his capable choices): in Ontario there is no general mandatory reporting duty for a capable older adult living in a private home, so the work is assessing capacity, addressing immediate safety, documenting and respecting his decisions. Adult protection legislation with broad mandatory reporting exists in Nova Scotia, Newfoundland and Labrador, and Prince Edward Island, which is why a national claim is false. Ontario's mandatory reporting duties attach to licensed long term care homes and to retirement homes, not to private residences. Financial exploitation should still be assessed and named, since it is common, frequently hidden, and among the most commonly reported forms.", "case_id": "C019", "case_text": "An 84-year-old man with moderate osteoarthritis and early Alzheimer disease lives with his son in Ontario.\nHe arrives for a routine visit noticeably thinner than at his last review, wearing soiled clothing, with bruises of different ages on both forearms.\nHis son answers every question for him and declines to leave the room. The community pharmacy reports that his medications have not been picked up for two months. He scores 21 on the MoCA.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 211, "domain": "assessment", "question": "He agrees to a plan including a community care assessment, weekly pharmacy delivery and review in two weeks. His son later tells you privately that he has not slept properly in months, has given up his job, and cannot manage any longer. What additional action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Assess caregiver strain and arrange respite and community support for the son", "B": "Advise the son that he is under investigation and should obtain legal counsel", "C": "Remove the son from all care decisions and appoint a substitute decision maker", "D": "Defer any support for the son until the abuse question is fully resolved"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess caregiver strain and arrange respite and community support for the son): caregiver burnout and abuse frequently share a household, and reducing strain is one of the few interventions that changes the conditions producing the harm. Framing the encounter as an investigation ends the only access you have to the home. A substitute decision maker is appointed only where capacity for the relevant decisions is absent, which has not been established. Withholding support until the abuse question resolves keeps the driving factor untouched while the older adult remains in the same home.", "case_id": "C019", "case_text": "An 84-year-old man with moderate osteoarthritis and early Alzheimer disease lives with his son in Ontario.\nHe arrives for a routine visit noticeably thinner than at his last review, wearing soiled clothing, with bruises of different ages on both forearms.\nHis son answers every question for him and declines to leave the room. The community pharmacy reports that his medications have not been picked up for two months. He scores 21 on the MoCA.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 219, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 31-year-old woman at 30 weeks gestation reports low mood and tearfulness for six weeks. She sleeps poorly, is constantly tired and has a variable appetite. You want a severity measure that will not be distorted by the normal physical changes of pregnancy. Which tool should you use?", "options": {"A": "The Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale, which omits somatic symptom items", "B": "The PHQ-9, which is validated for use throughout the antenatal period", "C": "The GAD-7, which captures the worry that accompanies low mood well", "D": "The Mood Disorder Questionnaire, designed to measure antenatal mood change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale, which omits somatic symptom items): it is used antenatally as well as postpartum, and leaving out sleep, appetite and energy items is exactly why it outperforms general depression scales during pregnancy. The PHQ-9 can be used in pregnancy, but its somatic items inflate scores when broken sleep and fatigue are near universal. A generalized anxiety measure tracks worry rather than depressive severity. The Mood Disorder Questionnaire screens for a lifetime history of hypomania or mania and measures nothing about current severity."}, {"id": 220, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 54-year-old woman completes the screening report shown before her appointment. She tells you her mood is reasonable and that she is coping, though she is exhausted most of the day. She has no psychiatric history. Based on the report, what is the most accurate interpretation of her total score?", "options": {"A": "The score confirms moderately severe depression and warrants starting an antidepressant", "B": "The score is invalid and the questionnaire should be repeated next month", "C": "Somatic items are inflating the score, so the total needs clinical interpretation", "D": "The zero on the ninth item establishes that she carries no suicide risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Somatic items are inflating the score, so the total needs clinical interpretation): the item detail shows that sleep, energy, appetite and concentration carry almost the whole total while the mood, interest and self worth items are low, which is the classic pattern in active cancer treatment. Treating the number as a diagnosis would start medication for a score driven by chemotherapy effects. The instrument is not invalid, and repeating it a month later would reproduce the same somatic inflation. A zero on the self harm item never clears a person of suicide risk, so that inference is unsafe as well as beside the point.", "image": "question_images/q_220.png"}, {"id": 223, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 24-year-old has had three depressive episodes since age 17 and has not responded to two adequate antidepressant trials at full dose. She is asking for a third medication today and is frustrated by the lack of progress. Before you prescribe, which additional screening is most likely to change your plan?", "options": {"A": "The AUDIT-C, to identify hazardous drinking that undermines antidepressant response", "B": "The GAD-7, to identify comorbid anxiety that would require a different agent", "C": "The Mood Disorder Questionnaire, to detect an unrecognized history of hypomania", "D": "The PHQ-9, to establish baseline severity before starting the next medication"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The Mood Disorder Questionnaire, to detect an unrecognized history of hypomania): early onset, recurrent episodes and poor antidepressant response are the exact indications for screening for a bipolar spectrum disorder before prescribing again, because an unrecognized diagnosis changes the entire treatment plan. Hazardous drinking does undermine response and is worth screening, but it would not overturn the medication class. Comorbid anxiety may guide the choice of agent without changing the underlying strategy. A severity measure documents a baseline and adds nothing about diagnosis."}, {"id": 228, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 74-year-old man who immigrated two years ago, speaks limited English and completed six years of formal schooling scores 19 on a MoCA administered in English by a receptionist. His adult daughter interpreted several of the items for him. How should you interpret this score?", "options": {"A": "It confirms mild cognitive impairment and justifies a memory clinic referral", "B": "It confirms dementia, since it falls well below the standard cut point", "C": "It is unreliable here, given language, education and the ad hoc interpreter", "D": "It becomes valid once one point is added for his limited formal education"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is unreliable here, given language, education and the ad hoc interpreter): language, literacy, education, sensory impairment and culture all shift cognitive screening scores, and administration in a second language through a family member compounds every one of those problems. The education adjustment adds a single point and cannot repair an invalid administration. Neither a diagnosis of mild cognitive impairment nor a diagnosis of dementia can rest on a test delivered under these conditions. Repeating the screen with a validated translation and a professional interpreter is the appropriate way forward."}, {"id": 231, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 79-year-old man has had six weeks of low mood, poor concentration and complaints of memory loss. He answers that he does not know to most cognitive questions, remains fully attentive throughout the interview, and dates the onset precisely to the week his wife died. Which conclusion is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Delirium, given the acute onset and the prominent cognitive complaints", "B": "Alzheimer disease, since memory complaints at this age are usually degenerative", "C": "Delirium superimposed on dementia, which is the commonest combination here", "D": "Depression, given preserved attention, a precise onset and memory complaints"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Depression, given preserved attention, a precise onset and memory complaints): depression in older adults can present with prominent cognitive complaints while attention and orientation stay intact, and the person typically emphasizes the deficit and gives do not know answers rather than confabulating. A sharply dateable onset tied to a bereavement fits a mood disorder, not a degenerative process. Delirium requires impaired and fluctuating attention, which he does not have. Alzheimer disease develops insidiously over months to years, and affected people often minimize rather than volunteer their memory problems."}, {"id": 247, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 41-year-old woman attends with recurrent abdominal pain. Her husband answers your questions, translates for her and refuses to step out of the room. She has attended three times this year with vague pain and once with a wrist injury. What is the most appropriate way to proceed?", "options": {"A": "Create a clinical reason to see her alone with a professional interpreter", "B": "Ask the abuse questions through her husband while watching her reaction closely", "C": "Defer the questions and book a longer appointment in three months time", "D": "Ask her to nod discreetly if she feels unsafe with him in the room"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Create a clinical reason to see her alone with a professional interpreter): repeated vague presentations, an injury and a companion who will not leave are recognized indicators, and the question must be asked in private through an interpreter who is not a family member. Using the suspected person as interpreter guarantees an unusable answer and can put her in danger. A coded signal asked in front of him is visible to him and unsafe. Postponing for three months leaves the risk unassessed and wastes the contact you already have."}, {"id": 248, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 33-year-old woman attends for follow up with the emergency department note shown. Her partner has again come to the appointment and has not left her side. She minimizes the incident and says that she bruises very easily. Which finding in the record most raises concern for future lethal violence?", "options": {"A": "The three day delay between the injury and her attendance at hospital", "B": "The old bruising over both upper arms noted on physical examination", "C": "The absence of any fracture on the facial radiographs that were performed", "D": "The neck petechiae, anterior neck tenderness and hoarse voice together"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The neck petechiae, anterior neck tenderness and hoarse voice together): that combination describes non fatal strangulation, which is one of the strongest known predictors of later homicide by an intimate partner. Delayed presentation and bruises of differing ages are genuine indicators of abuse but do not carry the same predictive weight for lethality. A normal radiograph reflects the imaging performed and says nothing about future danger. The immediate work is seeing her alone, assessing danger including weapons, threats to kill and stalking, then safety planning and documenting her own words.", "image": "question_images/q_248.png"}, {"id": 254, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 9-year-old is brought in with stomach aches every school morning that resolve completely on weekends. His teacher reports that he avoids written work and reads well below his class. His mother says he is bright at home. Vision and hearing were formally tested last month and were normal. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Reassure the family that the abdominal pain is functional and will settle", "B": "Refer for psychoeducational assessment and request school accommodations meanwhile", "C": "Order abdominal imaging and bloodwork before considering a school explanation", "D": "Repeat the vision and hearing testing before making any educational referral"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for psychoeducational assessment and request school accommodations meanwhile): a mismatch between apparent ability and academic output, avoidance of schoolwork and somatic complaints confined to school days is the recognized pattern of a specific learning disorder. Diagnosis requires psychoeducational assessment, and advocating for accommodation while the family waits is part of the primary care role. Reassurance alone leaves the child failing and the symptoms unexplained. Vision and hearing have already been excluded, and abdominal investigation before addressing an obvious school pattern subjects the child to unnecessary testing."}, {"id": 255, "domain": "assessment", "question": "A 71-year-old man scores 8 on the fifteen item Geriatric Depression Scale and reports low mood, fatigue and poor concentration over four months. He takes metformin and pantoprazole. His bloodwork is shown. What is the most appropriate next step before attributing his symptoms to depression?", "options": {"A": "Investigate and treat the vitamin B12 deficiency contributing to his presentation", "B": "Start an SSRI today and repeat the bloodwork again in three months", "C": "Treat the thyroid abnormality, which explains the fatigue and the low mood", "D": "Begin oral iron, since the anemia fully accounts for his current symptoms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Investigate and treat the vitamin B12 deficiency contributing to his presentation): every positive depression screen should be paired with a medical differential, and a low serum B12 with a raised mean corpuscular volume in a man taking metformin and a proton pump inhibitor is the abnormality that fits his symptoms. Thyroid function is within the reference range, so there is no thyroid abnormality to treat. Ferritin is normal and the red cells are macrocytic, so iron deficiency does not explain the anemia. Starting an antidepressant without addressing a treatable mimic risks months of ineffective treatment.", "image": "question_images/q_255.png"}, {"id": 278, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 51-year-old woman reports weight loss, heat intolerance and palpitations over four months. Her results are shown. She has been taking an over the counter supplement for energy that she bought online. Based on these results, which interpretation is correct and what does it imply for her presentation?", "options": {"A": "Primary hypothyroidism, requiring levothyroxine titrated to the stimulating hormone", "B": "Subclinical hyperthyroidism, warranting repeat testing before any treatment is considered", "C": "Sick euthyroid syndrome, expected during any significant intercurrent systemic illness", "D": "Overt hyperthyroidism, which her symptoms and the supplement history both support"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Overt hyperthyroidism, which her symptoms and the supplement history both support): a suppressed thyroid stimulating hormone of 0.02 mIU/L with a free T4 of 34 pmol/L and a free T3 of 12.5 pmol/L is biochemically overt hyperthyroidism, and it matches weight loss, heat intolerance and palpitations. Subclinical disease requires the free hormones to be normal, which they clearly are not. Hypothyroidism would show the stimulating hormone raised and the free T4 low, the opposite pattern. Unregulated online supplements can contain thyroid hormone, so the history matters and the source should be identified.", "image": "question_images/q_278.png"}, {"id": 282, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 58-year-old man has a non fasting lipid panel showing total cholesterol 6.4 mmol/L, HDL cholesterol 0.9 mmol/L and triglycerides 4.6 mmol/L. The laboratory has not reported an LDL value at all. He asks you what his LDL number is. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Use non HDL cholesterol of 5.5 mmol/L, since Friedewald is unreliable here", "B": "Calculate LDL as 3.4 mmol/L using the standard Friedewald estimation formula", "C": "Repeat the panel fasting, since non fasting sampling invalidates all lipid results", "D": "Report LDL as 1.6 mmol/L, which is at target for primary prevention"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use non HDL cholesterol of 5.5 mmol/L, since Friedewald is unreliable here): the Friedewald estimate becomes unreliable once triglycerides exceed roughly 4.5 mmol/L, which is why the laboratory suppressed the LDL value, and non HDL cholesterol of 6.4 minus 0.9 is a validated alternative that remains valid at any triglyceride level. Applying the formula anyway produces a number that looks precise and is not trustworthy. Non fasting sampling is accepted for routine Canadian lipid screening and does not invalidate the panel. The figure of 1.6 mmol/L cannot be derived from these numbers by any method."}, {"id": 283, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "An 84-year-old woman in long term care is more confused than usual for two days. She is afebrile, has no dysuria, frequency or suprapubic tenderness, and her chest is clear. A urine culture sent by nursing staff grows Escherichia coli at 10^8 CFU/L. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe nitrofurantoin for seven days and reassess her cognition afterward", "B": "Send a repeat culture and treat only if the same organism grows again", "C": "Start empiric ciprofloxacin, since delirium indicates systemic infection in older adults", "D": "Search for other causes of delirium rather than treating the bacteriuria"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Search for other causes of delirium rather than treating the bacteriuria): asymptomatic bacteriuria is common in older residents and should not be treated outside pregnancy and specified urological procedures, a Choosing Wisely Canada position, and she has no urinary symptoms or fever. Treating the culture is a classic diagnostic momentum error that closes the search while medication effects, constipation, pain, dehydration or infection elsewhere go unexamined. Repeating the culture answers a question that does not need answering and delays the real assessment. Antibiotics here add adverse effects and resistance without treating her delirium."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 71-year-old man taking metformin and pantoprazole for eight years reports numb feet and poor balance. His hemoglobin is 134 g/L with a mean corpuscular volume of 98 fL. Serum cobalamin is 178 pmol/L. What is the most appropriate next step in confirming the suspected diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Measure methylmalonic acid, since this cobalamin level is in the borderline range", "B": "Exclude deficiency, since the level and the mean corpuscular volume are normal", "C": "Order nerve conduction studies before pursuing any further biochemical testing", "D": "Begin intramuscular hydroxocobalamin and recheck the serum level in four weeks"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Measure methylmalonic acid, since this cobalamin level is in the borderline range): a level between roughly 150 and 220 pmol/L is indeterminate, and methylmalonic acid rises early in true tissue deficiency, so it resolves the ambiguity before committing to lifelong replacement. Neurological deficiency can occur with a normal hemoglobin and a normal cell volume, so a reassuring blood count does not exclude it, particularly with two drugs that reduce absorption. Nerve conduction studies describe the neuropathy but do not identify its cause. Treating first makes the confirmatory level uninterpretable afterward."}, {"id": 289, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 79-year-old woman on warfarin for a mechanical mitral valve has an INR of 6.4 drawn this morning. She has no bleeding, took an extra dose by mistake, and started clarithromycin four days ago for bronchitis. What is the most appropriate immediate management for her today?", "options": {"A": "Continue warfarin unchanged and recheck the INR in one week", "B": "Hold warfarin, stop clarithromycin and recheck the INR within 24 hours", "C": "Give intravenous vitamin K 10 mg and admit her for overnight observation", "D": "Reduce the weekly warfarin dose by ten percent and recheck in one month"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold warfarin, stop clarithromycin and recheck the INR within 24 hours): an INR above 5.0 is a critical result requiring same day action, and the correct step in a client who is not bleeding is to withhold warfarin, remove the interacting macrolide and recheck quickly. Clarithromycin inhibits warfarin metabolism, so leaving it in place lets the INR keep climbing whatever is done to the warfarin dose. Full dose intravenous vitamin K over corrects and risks valve thrombosis in a woman with a mechanical valve. Small dose reductions with a monthly recheck respond far too slowly to a value this high."}, {"id": 291, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has pleuritic chest pain. Her Wells score places the pre test probability of pulmonary embolism near 10 percent. A high sensitivity D dimer returns negative, with a negative likelihood ratio of 0.125. Which conclusion about her post test probability and next step is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 12 percent, so computed tomography pulmonary angiography is still required", "B": "About 5 percent, which still exceeds the accepted testing threshold in this case", "C": "About 1.4 percent, low enough to stop investigating for pulmonary embolism", "D": "Unable to calculate, because likelihood ratios apply only to positive test results"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 1.4 percent, low enough to stop investigating for pulmonary embolism): pre test odds of 0.1 divided by 0.9 are 0.111, and multiplying by 0.125 gives post test odds of 0.0139, which converts to a probability of roughly 1.4 percent, below the threshold at which further testing is justified. Imaging beyond this point exposes a young woman to contrast and radiation to chase a risk already excluded. Negative results cannot raise probability above the pre test value, so a higher post test figure is arithmetically impossible. Negative likelihood ratios exist precisely to quantify the value of a negative result."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "You order a screening test with 90 percent sensitivity and 90 percent specificity in a population where the condition affects 1 percent of people. A colleague argues that a positive result therefore means a 90 percent chance of disease. Which response best corrects this reasoning?", "options": {"A": "Sensitivity falls in low prevalence populations, so the estimate is too high", "B": "Specificity determines predictive value, so 90 percent is a reasonable estimate", "C": "Predictive value cannot be estimated without knowing the negative likelihood ratio", "D": "Positive predictive value is about 8 percent, because most positives are false"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Positive predictive value is about 8 percent, because most positives are false): in 10,000 people, 100 have the condition and 90 test positive, while 10 percent of the 9,900 without it produce 990 false positives, so 90 divided by 1,080 is about 8 percent. Sensitivity and specificity are properties of the test and stay roughly stable regardless of how common the condition is, which is exactly why prevalence must be supplied separately. Specificity alone cannot yield predictive value without knowing prevalence. The calculation shown needs only sensitivity, specificity and prevalence."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 68-year-old woman describes three weeks of new temporal headache, scalp tenderness when brushing her hair, and jaw ache while chewing. Her erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 88 mm/h. Her vision is currently normal. Which diagnostic priority should determine what you do at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Tension headache is most likely, so trial simple analgesia and review her", "B": "Await temporal artery biopsy results before committing to any specific treatment", "C": "Giant cell arteritis threatens vision, so start corticosteroids and refer urgently", "D": "Order neuroimaging first, since raised intracranial pressure must be excluded"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Giant cell arteritis threatens vision, so start corticosteroids and refer urgently): jaw claudication, scalp tenderness and a sedimentation rate of 88 mm/h in a woman over 65 make giant cell arteritis the must not miss diagnosis, and irreversible blindness can occur within days, so treatment begins before confirmation. Biopsy remains diagnostic and stays positive for one to two weeks after starting steroids, so waiting buys nothing and risks sight. Attributing a new headache with these specific features to tension type headache is premature closure. Raised intracranial pressure does not produce jaw claudication or this inflammatory picture."}, {"id": 297, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 58-year-old woman started methimazole six weeks ago and now has a sore throat and a temperature of 38.6 degrees Celsius. Her white cell count is 1.8 x 10^9/L with neutrophils 0.3 x 10^9/L. She looks unwell but is hemodynamically stable. What is the priority?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe oral amoxicillin and recheck the blood count in three days", "B": "Send her to hospital now for febrile neutropenia and stop methimazole", "C": "Reduce the methimazole dose and treat the sore throat symptomatically", "D": "Order a throat swab and await culture before making any antibiotic decision"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Send her to hospital now for febrile neutropenia and stop methimazole): a neutrophil count below 0.5 x 10^9/L with fever is a critical result requiring same day action, and antithyroid drug induced agranulocytosis can progress to overwhelming sepsis within hours. The offending drug must stop immediately rather than be reduced, because the reaction is idiosyncratic rather than dose dependent. Oral antibiotics prescribed from the community do not provide the broad spectrum intravenous cover and monitoring she needs. Waiting for a swab result treats a sore throat while ignoring the count that makes it dangerous."}, {"id": 299, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 55-year-old woman taking citalopram and domperidone has an ECG showing a QT interval of 480 ms at a heart rate of 60 beats per minute. She has no symptoms. Her potassium is 3.4 mmol/L and her magnesium 0.68 mmol/L. How should you interpret and act on this result?", "options": {"A": "The corrected QT is normal at this rate, so continue both medications unchanged", "B": "The corrected QT cannot be calculated without the RR interval measured in milliseconds", "C": "The corrected QT is 480 ms and prolonged, so review medications and correct electrolytes", "D": "The corrected QT is prolonged, so stop citalopram abruptly and repeat tomorrow"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The corrected QT is 480 ms and prolonged, so review medications and correct electrolytes): at a rate of 60 the RR interval is 1.0 second and its square root is 1.0, so the Bazett corrected value equals the measured 480 ms, which is prolonged and demands action. Both her drugs prolong repolarization and both hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia lower the threshold for torsades de pointes, so the electrolytes are part of the problem rather than incidental. Calling the value normal ignores a correction that happens to leave the number unchanged at this particular rate. Abrupt cessation of a serotonergic antidepressant causes a discontinuation syndrome and neglects the electrolytes entirely."}, {"id": 302, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "You are covering a colleague's practice for three weeks. A pathology result flagged abnormal was filed electronically two months ago with no documented action and no note to the client. Which feature of a safe results management system was missing in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Automatic laboratory flagging of results falling outside the reference interval", "B": "Restriction of result filing rights to administrative staff during locum coverage", "C": "Acknowledgement by a named clinician with a documented action and client notification", "D": "Routine repetition of every abnormal result before it is entered in the record"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledgement by a named clinician with a documented action and client notification): a closed loop system tracks each ordered test to a result, requires a named clinician to acknowledge it, and generates a documented action plus a documented message to the client, and every one of those steps failed here. The laboratory did flag the result, so flagging was present and by itself achieved nothing. Moving filing rights to administrative staff removes clinical accountability rather than adding it. Repeating every abnormal result before filing multiplies work without ever closing the loop with the person who needs to know."}, {"id": 318, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 61 year old woman reports three months of fatigue and constipation and the panel shown is returned. You notice that the first abnormality you saw seemed to explain her symptoms completely. Considering the panel as a whole rather than stopping there, what requires your attention first?", "options": {"A": "Start levothyroxine now and repeat thyroid function testing in eight weeks", "B": "Repeat the whole panel in three months, since all values are only borderline", "C": "Address the raised corrected calcium urgently, because it requires same day action", "D": "Refer for colonoscopy, because the hemoglobin is drifting downward toward anemia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Address the raised corrected calcium urgently, because it requires same day action): a corrected calcium above 3.0 mmol/L is a critical result demanding action on the day it is reported, whatever the client looks like. Fatigue and constipation fit hypercalcemia at least as well as they fit the thyroid result, so stopping at the first abnormality is the classic search satisficing error. A TSH of 9.8 with a free T4 inside the reference interval is subclinical and warrants a repeat rather than immediate treatment. A hemoglobin of 129 g/L is normal and generates no referral. The creatinine of 104 umol/L is mildly above the female reference interval and needs follow up, including a check for hypercalcemia related dehydration, but it is not the same day priority.", "image": "question_images/q_318.png"}, {"id": 323, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A new nurse practitioner recognizes streptococcal pharyngitis within seconds of seeing a client, before any conscious analysis. She then pauses because the same client also reports three weeks of drenching night sweats and unintended weight loss. What reasoning process should she now engage?", "options": {"A": "Continue with pattern recognition, since the first impression matched a stored illness script", "B": "Rely on the Centor score alone, since scoring tools reliably replace analytic reasoning", "C": "Switch to analytic reasoning, because key features sit outside the recognized script", "D": "Order broad investigations immediately, since intuition is unreliable in new practitioners"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Switch to analytic reasoning, because key features sit outside the recognized script): night sweats and weight loss over three weeks do not belong to the illness script for streptococcal pharyngitis, and an atypical feature is the signal to move deliberately from fast intuitive processing to slow hypothesis testing. Staying with the initial pattern would carry the mismatch forward unexamined. A scoring tool addresses only the probability of streptococcal infection and cannot evaluate the systemic features. Fast reasoning is accurate most of the time, so replacing it with reflex broad testing is neither necessary nor safe."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has three weeks of fatigue and joint aches. Your working diagnosis is a viral syndrome, but the picture is not yet clear and early inflammatory arthritis remains possible. She asks what is wrong with her. What is the most appropriate way to communicate at this point?", "options": {"A": "Name it a working diagnosis, say what would change it, and arrange review", "B": "Give the viral syndrome as a firm diagnosis to reassure her today", "C": "Withhold any diagnosis until every investigation has returned and been reviewed", "D": "List every condition still on the differential so she is fully informed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Name it a working diagnosis, say what would change it, and arrange review): naming the diagnosis as provisional is honest about where the reasoning actually stands, and it tells her the conclusion may move. Stating the findings that would change it gives her something concrete to watch for and makes her a participant in the diagnostic process. Booking review closes the loop so an evolving arthritis is caught rather than lost. Presenting an uncertain diagnosis as settled risks anchoring both of you, while refusing to say anything or reciting the whole differential leaves her with no usable plan."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A locum covered your practice for two weeks. On your return you find a chest radiograph report from that period describing a suspicious nodule, never opened and never actioned. Beyond contacting the client today, what change would most reliably prevent this category of error recurring?", "options": {"A": "Track every ordered test to a result acknowledged by a named responsible clinician", "B": "Ask the radiology department to telephone the practice with every abnormal report", "C": "Instruct clients to contact the clinic if they have not heard within two weeks", "D": "Review the electronic record more frequently during any period of locum coverage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Track every ordered test to a result acknowledged by a named responsible clinician): safety in results management comes from a closed loop system rather than from individual vigilance, and naming an accountable clinician for every pending result is what survives holidays and locum cover. The loop also requires a documented action and a documented message to the client. Depending on the laboratory or imaging service to telephone leaves the practice with no record of what remains outstanding. Putting the responsibility on clients assumes they know a test was ordered, and asking one person to look harder is not a system change."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "You assess a 58 year old with sudden severe chest pain, actively consider aortic dissection, judge it unlikely on specific grounds, and diagnose esophageal spasm. Considering what strong documentation of diagnostic reasoning requires, what should the note contain in addition to the final diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "The full list of every condition that this presenting symptom could possibly cause", "B": "A statement that dissection was excluded, without recording the supporting reasons", "C": "Only the positive findings, since negative findings lengthen the record unnecessarily", "D": "The differential considered, why dissection was judged unlikely, and the safety netting given"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The differential considered, why dissection was judged unlikely, and the safety netting given): a note recording the working diagnosis, the differential, the reasoning that ranked it, what was excluded and how, and the planned follow up is both better practice and stronger medicolegal protection than a clean note naming only the answer. A bare assertion that dissection was excluded records a conclusion without the evidence behind it. An exhaustive list of every possible cause is unusable and does not show reasoning. Pertinent negatives are exactly the findings that demonstrate the differential was worked through."}, {"id": 348, "domain": "diagnosis", "question": "A 38 year old woman has taken an antiseizure medication for seven years and has had three further collapses despite treatment. She now wishes to conceive. The archived consultation note that established her diagnosis is shown. What is the most appropriate first step now?", "options": {"A": "Ask what objective evidence established the original diagnosis, and arrange cardiac assessment", "B": "Increase the antiseizure medication dose, because the current dose is clearly inadequate", "C": "Switch her to a different antiseizure medication that is safer during early pregnancy", "D": "Accept the established diagnosis and refer her to obstetrics for pregnancy planning"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask what objective evidence established the original diagnosis, and arrange cardiac assessment): the note shows the label was applied clinically with no electroencephalography and no cardiac evaluation, and collapse while standing with brief jerking is a recognized presentation of arrhythmic or vasovagal syncope. Asking what evidence established an inherited diagnosis is the countermeasure for diagnostic momentum, and continued events despite treatment is itself a signal the diagnosis may be wrong. Increasing or switching the medication assumes the diagnosis is right. Referring for pregnancy planning without revisiting the label carries the original error into a pregnancy.", "image": "question_images/q_348.png"}, {"id": 367, "domain": "management", "question": "You review everything she is already doing for her knee before adding anything new. She has never mentioned her over the counter purchases to any clinician, and no one has asked. Which element of her current self management most needs your attention before you build the rest of the plan with her?", "options": {"A": "Her near daily oral ibuprofen use, which carries real risk at her age", "B": "Her levothyroxine dose, which may be contributing to her knee pain", "C": "The radiographic severity, which predicts how much pain she reports", "D": "Her body mass index of 34, which she has declined to discuss today"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Her near daily oral ibuprofen use, which carries real risk at her age): sustained over the counter oral anti inflammatory use in a woman of 61 risks gastrointestinal bleeding, kidney injury and rising blood pressure, and clients rarely report it because it is not a prescription. Reviewing it comes before layering anything new on top. Levothyroxine at a controlled dose does not cause knee pain. Radiographic severity correlates poorly with reported pain, and her weight is not the immediate safety concern.", "case_id": "C032", "case_text": "Priya Nagra, 61, has knee osteoarthritis, a body mass index of 34, and well controlled\nhypothyroidism. Radiographs show severe medial compartment narrowing. She takes\nlevothyroxine and buys ibuprofen over the counter, using it most days. Her creatinine is\n96 umol/L. She works as a school custodian and tells you her goal is to finish her shifts\nwithout limping, not to lose weight. She asks about a knee replacement.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 368, "domain": "management", "question": "She asks whether the severe narrowing seen on her radiograph means she should be sent for a knee replacement now. She has never tried a structured exercise programme, has not used a topical anti inflammatory, and has had no education about joint protection. How should you frame the decision about surgical referral?", "options": {"A": "Referral follows failure of conservative care, not the severity seen on imaging", "B": "Severe radiographic narrowing is now the accepted trigger for orthopedic surgical referral", "C": "Referral should wait until her body mass index falls below thirty", "D": "Surgical referral is reasonable now because her pain limits her work"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Referral follows failure of conservative care, not the severity seen on imaging): surgical opinion in knee osteoarthritis is triggered by persistent symptoms despite a genuine trial of exercise, education and appropriate analgesia, and she has had none of these. Radiographic narrowing correlates weakly with pain and function. Requiring a particular body mass index before referral is not a guideline standard and acts as a barrier. Sending her now skips the base of the treatment pyramid entirely.", "case_id": "C032", "case_text": "Priya Nagra, 61, has knee osteoarthritis, a body mass index of 34, and well controlled\nhypothyroidism. Radiographs show severe medial compartment narrowing. She takes\nlevothyroxine and buys ibuprofen over the counter, using it most days. Her creatinine is\n96 umol/L. She works as a school custodian and tells you her goal is to finish her shifts\nwithout limping, not to lose weight. She asks about a knee replacement.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 369, "domain": "management", "question": "You agree on a plan aimed at her stated goal of finishing her shifts without limping. She cannot afford a private physiotherapist and asks what she should use instead of the ibuprofen she has been taking most days. Which combination best fits her goal, her budget and her risk profile?", "options": {"A": "Regular acetaminophen plus a referral to a private physiotherapy clinic", "B": "Continued oral ibuprofen with a proton pump inhibitor added for protection", "C": "Topical anti inflammatory plus a community based supervised exercise programme", "D": "A short course of oral corticosteroid plus rest from her work shifts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Topical anti inflammatory plus a community based supervised exercise programme): topical agents give useful knee pain relief with far less systemic risk than oral anti inflammatories in an older adult, and exercise is the foundation of every osteoarthritis plan. A community programme is affordable where private physiotherapy is not. Adding gastric protection to daily oral ibuprofen still leaves the kidney and blood pressure risk untouched. Oral corticosteroid has no role here, and rest from work contradicts her stated goal.", "case_id": "C032", "case_text": "Priya Nagra, 61, has knee osteoarthritis, a body mass index of 34, and well controlled\nhypothyroidism. Radiographs show severe medial compartment narrowing. She takes\nlevothyroxine and buys ibuprofen over the counter, using it most days. Her creatinine is\n96 umol/L. She works as a school custodian and tells you her goal is to finish her shifts\nwithout limping, not to lose weight. She asks about a knee replacement.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 370, "domain": "management", "question": "At follow up her knee pain has improved and she is finishing her shifts without limping. She now asks you, without being prompted, whether her weight is making the knee worse and what she should do about it. Which response best reflects the Canadian obesity guideline approach?", "options": {"A": "Give her a target weight and a daily calorie limit to reach it", "B": "Reassure her that weight is not relevant now that her pain has improved", "C": "Refer her directly to a bariatric surgery assessment programme this week", "D": "Explore what she wants to change and agree on health focused goals"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explore what she wants to change and agree on health focused goals): the Canadian adult obesity guideline treats obesity as a chronic disease assessed by health impairment and function, with goals negotiated around health rather than a number on the scale. She opened the conversation, so it is welcome, but the content still has to be collaborative. Prescribing a target weight and calorie limit ignores root causes and reinforces weight bias. Dismissing her question shuts down a concern she raised, and surgical referral precedes any assessment of what she actually wants.", "case_id": "C032", "case_text": "Priya Nagra, 61, has knee osteoarthritis, a body mass index of 34, and well controlled\nhypothyroidism. Radiographs show severe medial compartment narrowing. She takes\nlevothyroxine and buys ibuprofen over the counter, using it most days. Her creatinine is\n96 umol/L. She works as a school custodian and tells you her goal is to finish her shifts\nwithout limping, not to lose weight. She asks about a knee replacement.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 377, "domain": "management", "question": "A 55 year old woman has an LDL cholesterol of 5.4 mmol/L, no cardiovascular disease, no diabetes, a blood pressure of 124/78 mmHg, and she does not smoke. She asks you to calculate her risk score first and to treat only if that score comes back high. How should you advise her?", "options": {"A": "Her risk score should be calculated and repeated annually before any treatment", "B": "Lifestyle change for six months is appropriate before a statin is considered", "C": "This LDL level is a statin indicated condition and bypasses risk calculation", "D": "Treatment is warranted only if her non HDL cholesterol also exceeds target"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (This LDL level is a statin indicated condition and bypasses risk calculation): Canadian Cardiovascular Society guidance names an LDL cholesterol of 5.0 mmol/L or higher as a statin indicated condition, so treatment is recommended without first calculating a Framingham score. Calculating one here would only delay a decision that has already been made. A six month lifestyle trial does not bring an LDL of this magnitude to target. Non HDL cholesterol is a secondary target and does not gate the initial decision."}, {"id": 390, "domain": "management", "question": "You are transferring a complex client's care to a new primary care provider in another city, and the receiving provider has accepted. You want the written handover to be safe rather than merely complete. Which element is most often omitted and most important to include in that document?", "options": {"A": "A full copy of every progress note written during your years of care", "B": "A list of preferred appointment times and the client's clinic contact details", "C": "Your personal impression of how motivated the client is to follow advice", "D": "Outstanding investigations with dates and who is responsible for the results"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Outstanding investigations with dates and who is responsible for the results): pending results are the classic item that falls between two providers, and a result nobody owns is the commonest source of harm at a handover. Naming the test, the date and the responsible person closes that gap explicitly. Sending every note buries the essential information instead of transmitting it. Appointment preferences are administrative, and a subjective judgement about motivation biases the receiving provider without adding clinical value."}, {"id": 395, "domain": "management", "question": "An 88 year old man with frailty and a life expectancy measured in months takes metformin alone, and his A1C is 7.8 percent. His family asks you to add insulin to bring the number closer to seven. He has no symptoms of hyperglycemia and has fallen twice this year. What is the most appropriate plan?", "options": {"A": "Add basal insulin at a low dose and titrate it slowly toward target", "B": "Add a sulfonylurea, which is cheaper and simpler than insulin for him", "C": "Stop the metformin entirely, since glucose control no longer matters here", "D": "Keep current therapy, explaining that a relaxed target fits his situation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep current therapy, explaining that a relaxed target fits his situation): Diabetes Canada sets an A1C target of 7.1 to 8.5 percent for frail older adults, and 7.1 to 8.0 percent for those who are functionally dependent, because hypoglycemia becomes the greater danger, and at 7.8 percent he already sits within that target. Intensifying therapy for a number in this setting buys no benefit and risks falls and hypoglycemia. Insulin and sulfonylureas are the two classes most likely to cause exactly that harm. Stopping metformin is unnecessary when it is tolerated and prevents symptomatic hyperglycemia."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "management", "question": "Your client with type 2 diabetes has an eGFR of 52 mL/min/1.73 m2 and an A1C of 8.3 percent on maximum tolerated metformin. She cannot pay out of pocket for anything. The coverage policy excerpt for the agent you want is shown. Based on it, what is the most appropriate action today?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe the agent now and tell her coverage will be applied retroactively", "B": "Choose a different class, since she does not meet the listed coverage criteria", "C": "Have her start the medication and submit the form if she tolerates it", "D": "Complete the special authority form now and diarize the decision date"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Complete the special authority form now and diarize the decision date): she meets the listed criterion of reduced kidney function with an A1C above target on metformin, so the form is the route to coverage she can actually use. Decisions take about fifteen business days, and tracking that date stops an application from quietly disappearing. Coverage is explicitly not retroactive, so telling her otherwise leaves her with a bill. Abandoning the indicated class or starting before approval both push cost back onto a client who cannot pay.", "image": "question_images/q_400.png"}, {"id": 403, "domain": "management", "question": "A 69 year old woman with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction records the home values shown over five weeks. She reports increasing ankle swelling but no chest pain and no fever. She takes furosemide, bisoprolol, ramipril and spironolactone. Based on the trend, what is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Increase the bisoprolol dose to control her rising resting heart rate", "B": "Increase the diuretic dose and review her again within a few days", "C": "Stop the ramipril, since a rising heart rate suggests it is poorly tolerated", "D": "Arrange an urgent echocardiogram before adjusting any of her medications"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Increase the diuretic dose and review her again within a few days): a steady gain of nearly five kilograms with a rising resting heart rate and falling oxygen saturation is progressive congestion, and congestion is treated with more diuretic plus close review. Early outpatient escalation is precisely what keeps this client out of hospital. Raising the beta blocker during decompensation reduces cardiac output and is contraindicated. The renin angiotensin blocker carries prognostic benefit and her pressure is stable, and echocardiography would not change today's action.", "image": "question_images/q_403.png"}, {"id": 410, "domain": "management", "question": "Which finding on your initial focused assessment would most strongly indicate that this man has sepsis rather than an uncomplicated urinary tract infection, and would therefore change your disposition decision today? Consider what best separates infection alone from infection with organ dysfunction.", "options": {"A": "Costovertebral angle tenderness with nitrites and leukocytes on urine dipstick testing", "B": "Temperature of 38.9 degrees Celsius with rigors and three days of dysuria", "C": "Cloudy strong smelling urine with urinary frequency and suprapubic discomfort reported", "D": "New confusion with hypotension and a respiratory rate above 22 breaths"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (New confusion with hypotension and a respiratory rate above 22 breaths): Sepsis is infection plus new organ dysfunction, and new confusion with hypotension and tachypnoea signals exactly that. Costovertebral angle tenderness with abnormal dipstick findings localizes the infection but says nothing about organ dysfunction. Fever with rigors and dysuria describes infection severity in symptom terms rather than physiological failure. Cloudy urine with frequency is common in uncomplicated cystitis and does not change disposition.", "case_id": "C034", "case_text": "A 78 year old man with type 2 diabetes is brought to your rural clinic by his daughter.\nFor three days he has had fever, back pain and burning on urination, and today he is\nconfused and unsteady on his feet. He normally lives alone and manages his own\nmedications. He takes metformin, ramipril and atorvastatin. The nearest emergency\ndepartment is 50 minutes away by road. His temperature is 38.9 degrees Celsius.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 411, "domain": "management", "question": "Review the recorded vital signs shown, taken on arrival and again 30 minutes later while intravenous access was being obtained. Based on the trend shown rather than any single reading, which conclusion should most influence your next decision for this man?", "options": {"A": "He is deteriorating physiologically and needs immediate transfer with fluid resuscitation started", "B": "He is febrile but hemodynamically stable enough for oral antibiotics and review", "C": "The readings reflect anxiety and pain, so analgesia should be given first", "D": "The changes fall within expected variation, so repeat the readings in an hour"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He is deteriorating physiologically and needs immediate transfer with fluid resuscitation started): Falling blood pressure, rising pulse, rising respiratory rate and deepening drowsiness across 30 minutes describe progressive physiological deterioration, which mandates resuscitation and transfer rather than observation. Calling him stable ignores the direction of travel in every recorded parameter. Anxiety and pain do not lower blood pressure while consciousness declines. Waiting an hour in a clinic 50 minutes from hospital risks arrival in refractory shock.", "case_id": "C034", "case_text": "A 78 year old man with type 2 diabetes is brought to your rural clinic by his daughter.\nFor three days he has had fever, back pain and burning on urination, and today he is\nconfused and unsteady on his feet. He normally lives alone and manages his own\nmedications. He takes metformin, ramipril and atorvastatin. The nearest emergency\ndepartment is 50 minutes away by road. His temperature is 38.9 degrees Celsius.", "case_size": 4, "image": "question_images/q_411.png"}, {"id": 412, "domain": "management", "question": "You have decided this man needs emergency transfer, and the ambulance is estimated at 25 minutes. Your clinic can draw blood, start intravenous fluids and give intravenous antibiotics. Which sequence of actions over the next few minutes best reflects Canadian sepsis care in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Await culture results before antibiotics, giving fluids and oxygen while arranging transport", "B": "Draw blood cultures, give broad spectrum antibiotics and a crystalloid bolus immediately", "C": "Give antibiotics only after hospital arrival so that cultures are not contaminated", "D": "Give a fluid bolus alone and defer antibiotics to the receiving physician"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Draw blood cultures, give broad spectrum antibiotics and a crystalloid bolus immediately): Canadian sepsis care in the first hour is cultures if they cause no delay, immediate broad spectrum antibiotics, lactate measurement and a crystalloid bolus for hypotension. Waiting for culture results before antibiotics costs hours, and mortality rises with every hour of delay. Deferring antibiotics until hospital arrival adds at least 50 minutes of untreated sepsis. Fluids alone address perfusion without treating the source driving the shock.", "case_id": "C034", "case_text": "A 78 year old man with type 2 diabetes is brought to your rural clinic by his daughter.\nFor three days he has had fever, back pain and burning on urination, and today he is\nconfused and unsteady on his feet. He normally lives alone and manages his own\nmedications. He takes metformin, ramipril and atorvastatin. The nearest emergency\ndepartment is 50 minutes away by road. His temperature is 38.9 degrees Celsius.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 413, "domain": "management", "question": "The paramedics arrive and ask for a handover. Your clinic is 50 minutes from the receiving hospital and you want the emergency team ready when he arrives. Which handover element is most likely to change what the receiving team actually does in the first hour?", "options": {"A": "A full past medical history including his previous cholecystectomy and hernia repair", "B": "His usual community pharmacy and the name of his regular family physician", "C": "The antibiotic given with its time, the fluid volume infused, and cultures drawn", "D": "A description of the daughter's concerns and her contact telephone number"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The antibiotic given with its time, the fluid volume infused, and cultures drawn): The receiving team's first hour decisions depend on what has already been given, so naming the antibiotic and its time, the fluid volume infused and whether cultures were drawn directly shapes whether they repeat, escalate or withhold treatment. Remote surgical history rarely alters immediate resuscitation. Pharmacy and family physician details matter for continuity rather than for the first hour. The daughter's contact information belongs in the record but does not change treatment.", "case_id": "C034", "case_text": "A 78 year old man with type 2 diabetes is brought to your rural clinic by his daughter.\nFor three days he has had fever, back pain and burning on urination, and today he is\nconfused and unsteady on his feet. He normally lives alone and manages his own\nmedications. He takes metformin, ramipril and atorvastatin. The nearest emergency\ndepartment is 50 minutes away by road. His temperature is 38.9 degrees Celsius.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 427, "domain": "management", "question": "You are called to the waiting room where a woman with type 1 diabetes is unresponsive and cannot be roused. Her capillary glucose reads 1.9 mmol/L. There is no intravenous access yet and her airway is patent. Which action is most appropriate right now?", "options": {"A": "Give intramuscular glucagon while a colleague attempts intravenous access for dextrose", "B": "Place glucose gel inside her cheek and monitor her level of consciousness", "C": "Give oral juice with support because she still has an intact gag reflex", "D": "Wait for emergency services since dextrose must be given by a physician"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give intramuscular glucagon while a colleague attempts intravenous access for dextrose): An unresponsive client must have nothing placed in the mouth, so glucagon is given while access is attempted and intravenous dextrose follows as soon as a line is available. Buccal gel still risks aspiration in someone who cannot be roused. Offering juice to an unresponsive client is unsafe regardless of gag reflex. Nurse practitioners may administer dextrose within scope, and waiting for transport prolongs cerebral hypoglycemia."}, {"id": 435, "domain": "management", "question": "A 5 year old boy has 18 hours of vomiting and watery non bloody diarrhea after a family gathering. He is alert with moist mucous membranes, brisk capillary refill and normal urine output. His mother asks for an antiemetic and an antibiotic. Which plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Give oral rehydration solution in small frequent amounts and provide clear return precautions", "B": "Prescribe azithromycin because the shared meal suggests a bacterial food borne illness", "C": "Prescribe loperamide to reduce fluid losses and shorten the duration of illness", "D": "Arrange intravenous rehydration in the clinic to prevent further deterioration today"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give oral rehydration solution in small frequent amounts and provide clear return precautions): Oral rehydration in small frequent volumes is the intervention for viral gastroenteritis in a well hydrated child, supported by clear advice on when to return. Azithromycin is not indicated for non bloody watery diarrhea without systemic features. Antimotility agents are avoided in children and are contraindicated with bloody diarrhea or high fever. Intravenous fluid is unnecessary when perfusion, mucous membranes and urine output are all normal."}, {"id": 437, "domain": "management", "question": "A 61 year old woman with cough and fever has the radiology report shown. In clinic she is alert, blood pressure 128/76 mmHg, heart rate 92, respiratory rate 20 and oxygen saturation 95 percent on room air. She lives with her daughter. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Admit her for intravenous antibiotics because lobar consolidation requires inpatient treatment", "B": "Withhold antibiotics and repeat the radiograph in two weeks to confirm resolution", "C": "Treat as an outpatient with oral antibiotics, review in 48 hours, give precautions", "D": "Arrange chest computed tomography to exclude an underlying obstructing lung lesion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat as an outpatient with oral antibiotics, review in 48 hours, give precautions): She has radiographically confirmed pneumonia with normal mental status, stable hemodynamics, adequate oxygenation and social support, which places her in the low risk group managed at home with oral antibiotics and a 48 hour review. Lobar consolidation by itself does not determine the site of care. Withholding antibiotics for confirmed pneumonia in a 61 year old is unsafe. Computed tomography is reserved for non resolving pneumonia rather than the initial episode.", "image": "question_images/q_437.png"}, {"id": 439, "domain": "management", "question": "You have completed the excision, achieved hemostasis, closed the wound with sutures and sent the specimen for pathology. The client is comfortable and preparing to leave. Which element of your closing plan most reduces the risk of a serious adverse outcome for him?", "options": {"A": "Written aftercare naming infection signs, who to call, and pathology follow up", "B": "A verbal reminder to keep the wound dry for the first 24 hours", "C": "A prescription for prophylactic oral antibiotics covering the whole healing period", "D": "A suture removal appointment that the client books himself at reception today"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Written aftercare naming infection signs, who to call, and pathology follow up): Most serious harm after minor surgery comes from unrecognized infection or a lost pathology result, so written aftercare that names infection signs, gives a named contact and states when results will be discussed addresses both risks. Verbal advice about keeping the wound dry is useful but easily forgotten and covers only one issue. Prophylactic antibiotics after a clean minor excision cause harm without benefit. Leaving the client to book suture removal risks it never happening."}, {"id": 449, "domain": "management", "question": "A 70 year old man reports six weeks of fatigue and dark stools and takes daily acetylsalicylic acid. He is hemodynamically stable in clinic today. Review his results shown. Which interpretation best explains the pattern and should direct your next step?", "options": {"A": "Anemia of chronic disease requiring investigation for an underlying inflammatory condition", "B": "Chronic occult gastrointestinal blood loss requiring urgent endoscopy and iron replacement", "C": "Hemolysis requiring a reticulocyte count, bilirubin and peripheral blood film review", "D": "Dietary iron deficiency requiring nutritional counselling and repeat testing in three months"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Chronic occult gastrointestinal blood loss requiring urgent endoscopy and iron replacement): Microcytic anemia with a ferritin of 6 ug/L, reactive thrombocytosis and a raised urea against a normal creatinine is the classic pattern of gastrointestinal blood loss with digested blood in the gut, so endoscopy and iron replacement are both needed. Anemia of chronic disease preserves or raises ferritin rather than depleting it. Hemolysis neither depletes iron stores nor raises urea selectively. Attributing profound iron deficiency in a 70 year old to diet alone risks missing malignancy.", "image": "question_images/q_449.png"}, {"id": 456, "domain": "management", "question": "A 19 year old with type 1 diabetes has vomiting, Kussmaul breathing and a capillary glucose of 29 mmol/L. Blood pressure is 96/58 mmHg with a heart rate of 128. Transfer is arranged for 40 minutes from now. Which intervention should you begin first in your clinic?", "options": {"A": "A subcutaneous rapid acting insulin bolus to begin correcting the hyperglycemia now", "B": "Intravenous isotonic crystalloid to restore circulating volume before any insulin therapy", "C": "Intravenous sodium bicarbonate to correct the metabolic acidosis before transport begins", "D": "Oral rehydration solution in small volumes to avoid triggering any further vomiting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous isotonic crystalloid to restore circulating volume before any insulin therapy): Diabetic ketoacidosis is a profoundly volume depleted state, and isotonic crystalloid restores perfusion, lowers glucose and improves acidosis before insulin is started. Giving insulin first with unreplaced volume worsens hypotension and drives potassium intracellularly. Bicarbonate is not used for the acidosis of ketoacidosis at this level. Oral fluids cannot correct a deficit of several litres in a vomiting client."}, {"id": 461, "domain": "management", "question": "You have twenty minutes with her and must decide where to begin. She has no chest pain, her lungs are clear on auscultation and her weight is unchanged. Which action will most directly establish whether her new symptoms represent a medication induced problem rather than worsening heart failure?", "options": {"A": "Arrange an urgent echocardiogram to reassess her left ventricular ejection fraction this week", "B": "Measure a brain natriuretic peptide level and repeat the value in seven days", "C": "Order serum creatinine and electrolytes and compare with her results from three months ago", "D": "Refer her to cardiology today for assessment of decompensated heart failure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Order serum creatinine and electrolytes and compare with her results from three months ago): Her new symptoms began two weeks after a nonsteroidal anti inflammatory drug was added to an angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor and a thiazide, so renal function and potassium are the measurements that separate drug induced acute kidney injury from progression of heart failure. Comparison against a recent baseline is what makes the result interpretable. Echocardiography and natriuretic peptide testing address ventricular function, which her clear lung fields and stable weight do not suggest has changed. Urgent referral before any bloodwork skips the cheapest and most informative test available today.", "case_id": "C037", "case_text": "An 82-year-old woman with hypertension and heart failure attends for routine review.\nHer regular medications are ramipril 10 mg daily, hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily and atorvastatin 20 mg daily.\nTwo weeks ago she began buying ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily from the pharmacy for low back pain.\nShe reports reduced urine output, ankle swelling and mild nausea.\nHer blood pressure today is 104/62 mmHg and she weighs 54 kg.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 462, "domain": "management", "question": "Her bloodwork drawn this morning is shown. Three months ago her creatinine was 82 umol/L and her potassium was 4.4 mmol/L. She has missed no doses of her regular medications and has eaten normally. Which explanation best accounts for the pattern of change seen in these results?", "options": {"A": "Progressive heart failure has reduced renal perfusion despite unchanged medication doses", "B": "Combined renin angiotensin blockade, diuretic and nonsteroidal drug have collapsed glomerular filtration", "C": "Hydrochlorothiazide has caused hyponatremia with a secondary rise in serum creatinine", "D": "Atorvastatin induced rhabdomyolysis is raising her creatinine and releasing intracellular potassium stores"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Combined renin angiotensin blockade, diuretic and nonsteroidal drug have collapsed glomerular filtration): This is the triple whammy: the nonsteroidal drug blocks prostaglandin mediated dilation of the afferent arteriole, the angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor prevents efferent constriction, and the thiazide reduces circulating volume, so filtration falls and potassium rises. The timing, two weeks after the only new agent, points to drugs rather than to disease progression. Heart failure progression would be expected to produce raised venous pressure and crackles, which she does not have. Statin myopathy causes muscle pain with a raised creatine kinase rather than this renal picture.", "case_id": "C037", "case_text": "An 82-year-old woman with hypertension and heart failure attends for routine review.\nHer regular medications are ramipril 10 mg daily, hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily and atorvastatin 20 mg daily.\nTwo weeks ago she began buying ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily from the pharmacy for low back pain.\nShe reports reduced urine output, ankle swelling and mild nausea.\nHer blood pressure today is 104/62 mmHg and she weighs 54 kg.", "case_size": 4, "image": "question_images/q_462.png"}, {"id": 463, "domain": "management", "question": "She is alert, her chest is clear and she is passing small volumes of concentrated urine. Her electrocardiogram shows no peaked T waves. You decide to act on the medication list before arranging any further investigation. Which change should you make first, and on which agents?", "options": {"A": "Stop the ibuprofen and hold both the ramipril and the hydrochlorothiazide", "B": "Halve the ramipril dose and continue the ibuprofen with gastric protection", "C": "Stop the hydrochlorothiazide only and add an oral potassium binding agent", "D": "Continue all medications and recheck her renal panel in seven days"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the ibuprofen and hold both the ramipril and the hydrochlorothiazide): All three agents drive the same mechanism, so the nonsteroidal drug she added stops permanently while the two prescribed agents are held until filtration recovers. Removing only one arm of the mechanism leaves the injury in place. Gastric protection does nothing for the kidney, because this injury is hemodynamic rather than mucosal. Waiting a week with a potassium of 5.7 mmol/L and an eGFR of 24 mL/min/1.73m2 risks arrhythmia and further loss of function.", "case_id": "C037", "case_text": "An 82-year-old woman with hypertension and heart failure attends for routine review.\nHer regular medications are ramipril 10 mg daily, hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily and atorvastatin 20 mg daily.\nTwo weeks ago she began buying ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily from the pharmacy for low back pain.\nShe reports reduced urine output, ankle swelling and mild nausea.\nHer blood pressure today is 104/62 mmHg and she weighs 54 kg.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 464, "domain": "management", "question": "Two days after the medication changes her potassium is 4.8 mmol/L and her creatinine has fallen to 121 umol/L. She remains at home with family support, eats normally and asks what happens next with her blood pressure and heart failure treatment. Which follow-up plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Leave both agents stopped permanently and manage her blood pressure with amlodipine", "B": "Restart both ramipril and hydrochlorothiazide today and recheck bloodwork in three months", "C": "Substitute an angiotensin receptor blocker for ramipril and recheck bloodwork in three months", "D": "Restart ramipril alone once renal function stabilises and recheck bloodwork in two weeks"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Restart ramipril alone once renal function stabilises and recheck bloodwork in two weeks): Ramipril has documented mortality benefit in heart failure and is reintroduced once filtration has recovered, with creatinine and potassium rechecked one to two weeks after the restart rather than at a distant interval. Reintroducing one agent at a time keeps any further deterioration attributable. Abandoning renin angiotensin blockade permanently removes proven benefit when the reversible cause was an over the counter drug she bought herself. Switching to a receptor blocker preserves the identical hemodynamic effect, so it does not remove the risk.", "case_id": "C037", "case_text": "An 82-year-old woman with hypertension and heart failure attends for routine review.\nHer regular medications are ramipril 10 mg daily, hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily and atorvastatin 20 mg daily.\nTwo weeks ago she began buying ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily from the pharmacy for low back pain.\nShe reports reduced urine output, ankle swelling and mild nausea.\nHer blood pressure today is 104/62 mmHg and she weighs 54 kg.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 494, "domain": "management", "question": "A 66-year-old man with heart failure takes ramipril and spironolactone. He mentions that he has switched to a potassium based salt substitute and started an over the counter supplement recommended to him for muscle cramps. He feels generally well today. Which action is the priority?", "options": {"A": "Stop the spironolactone immediately and review him again in four weeks", "B": "Advise him to continue the salt substitute, since sodium reduction helps", "C": "Measure serum potassium and creatinine before making any other change", "D": "Add a loop diuretic prophylactically to increase urinary potassium excretion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Measure serum potassium and creatinine before making any other change): Renin angiotensin blockade combined with a potassium sparing diuretic already raises potassium, and a potassium based salt substitute plus a supplement adds two further loads that he does not think of as medication. Hyperkalemia is usually silent until it causes an arrhythmia, so feeling well is not reassurance. Measurement precedes any prescribing change because the result determines whether this is an emergency. Stopping a mortality reducing heart failure drug or adding a loop diuretic blindly are both premature without a potassium value."}, {"id": 500, "domain": "management", "question": "A 75-year-old man started amlodipine 10 mg daily three months ago and now has bilateral pitting ankle edema. He has no orthopnoea, no raised jugular venous pressure and clear lung fields, and his weight is unchanged. A colleague suggests adding furosemide. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Add low dose furosemide each morning and reassess the edema in two weeks", "B": "Order an echocardiogram before making any change to his current medications", "C": "Prescribe compression stockings and continue the antihypertensive at its current dose", "D": "Reduce or change the calcium channel blocker rather than adding a diuretic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce or change the calcium channel blocker rather than adding a diuretic): Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers cause dose dependent peripheral edema through arteriolar dilation rather than fluid overload, and the absence of raised venous pressure, orthopnoea and weight gain argues strongly against heart failure. Treating that edema with a diuretic is the textbook prescribing cascade and exposes him to volume depletion and electrolyte loss without touching the cause. Reducing the dose or moving to another antihypertensive class resolves it. Imaging is not required when the temporal link and the examination findings are this clear."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "management", "question": "A 34-year-old woman needs treatment for a moderate skin and soft tissue infection. Her chart records a penicillin allergy from childhood, which she describes as a rash while taking amoxicillin at age six, with no swelling, no breathing difficulty and no hospital visit. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe vancomycin, because beta lactams are now contraindicated for life", "B": "Prescribe a fluoroquinolone to avoid any possibility of cross reactivity", "C": "Repeat the amoxicillin now and observe her in the clinic for one hour", "D": "Take a detailed allergy history and consider a beta lactam based on it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take a detailed allergy history and consider a beta lactam based on it): Most childhood rashes labelled as penicillin allergy are not immunoglobulin E mediated, and reflexive avoidance drives the use of broader, more toxic and often less effective agents. Structured history taking that covers the reaction, its timing and its severity often allows safe beta lactam use or referral for testing. Reserving glycopeptides and fluoroquinolones for people who genuinely need them is core antimicrobial stewardship. Rechallenging with the original drug before taking that history skips the assessment that would make the decision safe."}, {"id": 510, "domain": "management", "question": "A 57-year-old woman has taken 120 morphine milligram equivalents daily for six years for chronic non cancer pain, with only modest functional benefit. She is stable, engaged in her care, and agrees to a trial of reduction. Which tapering approach is most consistent with Canadian guidance?", "options": {"A": "Reduce by 25 percent weekly until she reaches 50 morphine milligram equivalents", "B": "Stop the opioid now and manage her withdrawal with clonidine over two weeks", "C": "Reduce by 5 to 10 percent every two to four weeks, slowing at lower doses", "D": "Maintain the current dose indefinitely, since tapering above 90 is considered unsafe"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce by 5 to 10 percent every two to four weeks, slowing at lower doses): Canadian guidance supports a slow taper of roughly 5 to 10 percent of the morphine equivalent dose every two to four weeks, going slower as the dose falls, and pausing or reversing the taper if the person destabilises. Rapid or forced tapering causes harm, including withdrawal, loss of function and transition to the unregulated drug supply. Abrupt cessation with a withdrawal agent is not a taper and does nothing for her pain. Trials of tapering are recommended above 90 morphine milligram equivalents rather than avoided."}, {"id": 511, "domain": "management", "question": "A 38-year-old woman taking a combined oral contraceptive has begun St John's wort bought at a health food store for low mood, and she does not regard it as a medication. She asks whether anything about her contraception needs to change. Which counselling point is most important?", "options": {"A": "The herbal product is pharmacologically inert and needs no change to her contraception", "B": "She should double her contraceptive dose for as long as she takes the herbal product", "C": "The combination raises contraceptive concentrations and substantially increases thrombotic risk", "D": "The herbal product can reduce contraceptive effectiveness through enzyme induction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The herbal product can reduce contraceptive effectiveness through enzyme induction): St John's wort is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 and of intestinal transport, accelerating clearance of ethinyl oestradiol and progestin, and it has produced breakthrough bleeding and unintended pregnancy. Natural health products belong in the Best Possible Medication History precisely because people do not report them as medications. Doubling a contraceptive dose is not an evidence based workaround and is not how this interaction is managed. Induction lowers rather than raises hormone concentrations."}, {"id": 524, "domain": "management", "question": "You want to know whether the zopiclone is still doing anything useful and what else is driving her poor sleep. She lives alone and drinks two glasses of wine most evenings. Which assessment would contribute most to the decision about continuing this medication?", "options": {"A": "A sleep history covering timing, alcohol, caffeine, naps and daytime function", "B": "An overnight polysomnography study to document her total sleep time objectively", "C": "A serum zopiclone concentration to confirm she takes the prescribed dose", "D": "Repeat cognitive testing in three months before considering any medication change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A sleep history covering timing, alcohol, caffeine, naps and daytime function): the decision turns on whether the drug still delivers benefit and what modifiable factors are keeping her awake, and evening alcohol is a strong candidate here. Sleep laboratory testing is reserved for suspected sleep apnea or parasomnia rather than for chronic hypnotic use with obvious behavioural contributors. Serum concentrations of this drug are not used clinically and would not tell you whether it helps. Deferring any change for three months of cognitive testing leaves her exposed after an injurious fall.", "case_id": "C044", "case_text": "Mrs. Belanger is 78 years old and has taken zopiclone 7.5 mg at bedtime for four years, originally prescribed during a bereavement. She now sleeps poorly despite the medication and feels groggy each morning.\nShe fell in the bathroom six weeks ago and sustained a wrist fracture. Her other medications are levothyroxine, calcium with vitamin D, and cetirizine for seasonal rhinitis. Her Mini Mental State Examination score is 27 out of 30 and her serum creatinine is 96 umol/L.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 525, "domain": "management", "question": "She says the tablet no longer helps her sleep but she is frightened of what will happen if she stops it. You weigh the ongoing benefit against the accumulating harm before recommending anything. Which feature of her presentation most strongly indicates that harm now outweighs benefit?", "options": {"A": "She has taken the medication continuously for four years without any review", "B": "Morning grogginess with a fall and fracture while the sleep benefit has gone", "C": "Her Mini Mental State Examination score of 27 shows early cognitive decline", "D": "Her age of 78 places the drug on the Beers Criteria list automatically"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Morning grogginess with a fall and fracture while the sleep benefit has gone): a documented harm has already occurred, the mechanism linking it to the drug is residual sedation, and the benefit side of the equation is now empty. Long duration without review is a trigger to reassess rather than proof that the balance has tipped. A score of 27 out of 30 sits at the borderline and cannot by itself be called decline without a previous score to compare. Appearing on a potentially inappropriate medication list flags the drug for review but does not measure harm in this individual.", "case_id": "C044", "case_text": "Mrs. Belanger is 78 years old and has taken zopiclone 7.5 mg at bedtime for four years, originally prescribed during a bereavement. She now sleeps poorly despite the medication and feels groggy each morning.\nShe fell in the bathroom six weeks ago and sustained a wrist fracture. Her other medications are levothyroxine, calcium with vitamin D, and cetirizine for seasonal rhinitis. Her Mini Mental State Examination score is 27 out of 30 and her serum creatinine is 96 umol/L.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 526, "domain": "management", "question": "She agrees to try coming off zopiclone and asks how quickly it can be done. She is worried about rebound insomnia and about withdrawal symptoms. You plan the taper together and arrange support for the sleep problem itself. Which plan best matches the Canadian deprescribing algorithm?", "options": {"A": "Stop zopiclone tonight and substitute trazodone 50 mg at bedtime instead", "B": "Halve the dose weekly for two weeks and then stop, without any other support", "C": "Reduce by about one quarter every two weeks, adding cognitive behavioural therapy", "D": "Switch to lorazepam 1 mg nightly and taper that agent over three months"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce by about one quarter every two weeks, adding cognitive behavioural therapy): the benzodiazepine receptor agonist algorithm pairs a slow stepwise reduction, with smaller decrements near the end, with the non pharmacological treatment that actually works for chronic insomnia. Substituting another sedating drug replaces one hypnotic with another and abandons the goal of the exercise. Halving weekly is faster than the algorithm advises and offers nothing to manage the insomnia that surfaces during withdrawal. Converting to a longer acting benzodiazepine adds an unnecessary switch and keeps her on a falls risk drug for months.", "case_id": "C044", "case_text": "Mrs. Belanger is 78 years old and has taken zopiclone 7.5 mg at bedtime for four years, originally prescribed during a bereavement. She now sleeps poorly despite the medication and feels groggy each morning.\nShe fell in the bathroom six weeks ago and sustained a wrist fracture. Her other medications are levothyroxine, calcium with vitamin D, and cetirizine for seasonal rhinitis. Her Mini Mental State Examination score is 27 out of 30 and her serum creatinine is 96 umol/L.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 527, "domain": "management", "question": "Two weeks into the taper she reports more awakenings, mild anxiety and irritability, but no tremor, no confusion and no seizure activity. She wants to know whether she should give up and go back to the original nightly dose. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Return to the original dose and plan to reattempt the taper next year", "B": "Continue reducing on schedule and add lorazepam as needed for the anxiety", "C": "Hold at the current dose, normalize the symptoms, and resume reducing when settled", "D": "Stop the remaining dose now, since withdrawal symptoms are already established"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold at the current dose, normalize the symptoms, and resume reducing when settled): mild rebound insomnia and irritability are expected, time limited and best managed by pausing rather than abandoning or accelerating the plan. Returning to the starting dose teaches her that the taper failed and makes the next attempt harder. Adding a benzodiazepine during a benzodiazepine receptor agonist taper reintroduces the exact class being withdrawn. Stopping the remaining dose abruptly is the maneuver that risks seizure and severe withdrawal, which is why the algorithm insists on gradual reduction.", "case_id": "C044", "case_text": "Mrs. Belanger is 78 years old and has taken zopiclone 7.5 mg at bedtime for four years, originally prescribed during a bereavement. She now sleeps poorly despite the medication and feels groggy each morning.\nShe fell in the bathroom six weeks ago and sustained a wrist fracture. Her other medications are levothyroxine, calcium with vitamin D, and cetirizine for seasonal rhinitis. Her Mini Mental State Examination score is 27 out of 30 and her serum creatinine is 96 umol/L.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 535, "domain": "management", "question": "A 77-year-old man returns for routine review feeling tired with reduced urine output over the past ten days. He has started a new over the counter product for back pain. His medication list is shown. Which combination on this list best explains his presentation?", "options": {"A": "Metformin with hydrochlorothiazide, producing lactate accumulation and fatigue", "B": "Pantoprazole with perindopril, producing hyponatremia and reduced urine output", "C": "Ibuprofen with pantoprazole, producing occult gastrointestinal blood loss", "D": "Perindopril, hydrochlorothiazide and ibuprofen, producing acute kidney injury"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Perindopril, hydrochlorothiazide and ibuprofen, producing acute kidney injury): the nonsteroidal drug blocks prostaglandin mediated dilation of the afferent arteriole, the renin angiotensin blocker prevents efferent constriction, and the diuretic reduces circulating volume, so glomerular filtration collapses. The timing fits exactly, because the third agent was bought without a prescription ten days ago. Lactate accumulation from metformin presents with acidosis and is a consequence of failing kidneys here rather than the initiating combination. Occult bleeding would present with anemia rather than falling urine output, and acid suppression does not reduce filtration.", "image": "question_images/q_535.png"}, {"id": 553, "domain": "management", "question": "A frail 88-year-old woman weighing 42 kg is being assessed for apixaban after a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation. Her serum creatinine is 62 umol/L and the laboratory reports an eGFR above 60 mL/min/1.73m2. Which statement should most influence your dosing decision?", "options": {"A": "Low muscle mass makes the reported eGFR overestimate her true drug clearance", "B": "The reported eGFR is the correct value to use for anticoagulant dose selection", "C": "Her creatinine is within range, so no dose adjustment could possibly be needed", "D": "Apixaban requires no renal assessment because it is minimally cleared renally"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Low muscle mass makes the reported eGFR overestimate her true drug clearance): creatinine production depends on muscle, so a frail 42 kg woman can have a reassuring creatinine alongside badly impaired clearance. Direct oral anticoagulant dosing in Canada uses creatinine clearance from the Cockcroft and Gault equation with actual body weight, which is not the same number the laboratory reports. Advanced age and low body weight are themselves dose reduction criteria for this agent and must be applied exactly rather than approximated. All agents in the class need renal function checked at least annually."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "management", "question": "You increase levothyroxine from 75 to 100 micrograms daily for a 55-year-old woman whose thyroid stimulating hormone was elevated with persistent fatigue. She asks when the blood test should be repeated and what you will do with the result. Which plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Repeat thyroid stimulating hormone in two weeks and adjust by symptoms alone", "B": "Repeat free thyroxine weekly until her fatigue has completely resolved", "C": "Repeat thyroid stimulating hormone in six to eight weeks and titrate to it", "D": "Repeat thyroid stimulating hormone in six months regardless of her symptoms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat thyroid stimulating hormone in six to eight weeks and titrate to it): for a thyroid replacement change both effectiveness and safety are read from the same value, and it must be drawn only after a new steady state has been reached. Testing at two weeks samples a hormone that has not yet equilibrated and invites an unnecessary further change. Weekly free thyroxine measurement tracks a parameter that is neither the titration target nor a measure of her symptom. Waiting six months leaves her either undertreated or overtreated for months, with the bone and atrial fibrillation risks that overtreatment carries."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "management", "question": "A 54-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis takes methotrexate 15 mg once weekly with folic acid. She develops a urinary tract infection and a colleague suggests trimethoprim with sulfamethoxazole. Her renal function is normal. Which consideration should determine the antibiotic choice here?", "options": {"A": "Trimethoprim shortens the methotrexate half life and reduces its effectiveness", "B": "Methotrexate should be switched to daily dosing during any acute infection", "C": "Folic acid fully protects against any interaction with antifolate antibiotics", "D": "Combined antifolate effects can cause severe bone marrow suppression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Combined antifolate effects can cause severe bone marrow suppression): both drugs interfere with folate pathways and the combination has produced fatal pancytopenia, so a different antibiotic such as nitrofurantoin should be chosen for uncomplicated cystitis. The interaction increases rather than decreases methotrexate exposure, partly through reduced renal tubular secretion. Converting a weekly antirheumatic dose to daily dosing is the classic fatal medication error with this drug and must never be done. Weekly folic acid reduces mucosal and hepatic adverse effects but does not neutralize this interaction."}, {"id": 560, "domain": "management", "question": "A 70-year-old man takes eight regular medications, all with a current indication, correct doses for his renal function, no duplications and no interactions. He is adherent and has no adverse effects. A student asks whether this counts as problematic polypharmacy. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because five or more medications always defines problematic polypharmacy", "B": "Yes, because eight medications guarantees an interaction he has not yet noticed", "C": "No, because appropriateness matters more than the number of medications", "D": "No, because polypharmacy applies only to adults aged 85 and older"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because appropriateness matters more than the number of medications): the count of five or more is a screening trigger for review rather than a diagnosis, and a regimen where every drug has a current indication, a correct dose and no interaction is appropriate polypharmacy. Treating the number as the problem leads to withdrawing beneficial therapy, which is the mirror image error the START criteria were written to prevent. Interactions are identified by review rather than assumed from a count. The concept applies across adult ages and is not restricted to the very old."}, {"id": 562, "domain": "management", "question": "A 32-year-old man with schizophrenia has been stable on olanzapine for two years. He has gained 14 kg since starting and his waist circumference has increased. He asks what tests he needs. Which monitoring set is most appropriate for the safety half of his plan?", "options": {"A": "Serum olanzapine concentration and complete blood count every three months", "B": "Liver enzymes and creatine kinase measured at each scheduled clinic appointment", "C": "Thyroid stimulating hormone and vitamin B12 measured twice in each year", "D": "Weight, blood pressure, fasting glucose and lipid profile at set intervals"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weight, blood pressure, fasting glucose and lipid profile at set intervals): the dominant long term harm of second generation antipsychotics is metabolic, and this drug carries the highest risk in the class, so anthropometric and metabolic parameters are what the safety plan must track. Serum concentrations are not used to guide therapy with this agent, and routine hematology monitoring belongs to clozapine. Liver and muscle enzymes are not the recurring toxicities here. Thyroid and vitamin B12 testing addresses neither the drug nor the weight gain pattern described."}, {"id": 567, "domain": "management", "question": "An 84-year-old woman has had three falls in six months. She takes amitriptyline for insomnia, temazepam as needed, bisoprolol, and tamsulosin for nocturia. Her postural blood pressure drop is 26 mmHg systolic. Which change should you prioritize to reduce her falls risk?", "options": {"A": "Stop bisoprolol, the only medication that lowers her resting heart rate", "B": "Add midodrine to correct the postural drop in her blood pressure", "C": "Stop amitriptyline first, then plan a taper of the temazepam", "D": "Reduce tamsulosin and continue her other three medications unchanged"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop amitriptyline first, then plan a taper of the temazepam): a tricyclic used for insomnia has no good indication, and it contributes sedation, anticholinergic burden and orthostatic hypotension at once, so it is both the most harmful and the easiest to remove. The benzodiazepine follows, tapered rather than stopped, because abrupt cessation risks withdrawal. Beta blockade may be treating a cardiac indication and is not the leading contributor to a postural drop of this size. Adding a pressor drug to counteract an effect caused by other drugs would start a new cascade."}, {"id": 568, "domain": "management", "question": "A 66-year-old man taking metoprolol 100 mg twice daily for angina decides on his own to stop all medications after reading about deprescribing. He telephones to tell you before doing so. Which counselling point is the most important to give him first?", "options": {"A": "All of his medications can be stopped together safely if he feels well", "B": "Abrupt beta blocker withdrawal can trigger angina, arrhythmia or infarction", "C": "Deprescribing is only appropriate for adults older than 75 years of age", "D": "He should stop half of each tablet dose starting on the same day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Abrupt beta blocker withdrawal can trigger angina, arrhythmia or infarction): receptor upregulation during chronic blockade means sudden cessation produces rebound sympathetic activity, and in someone with angina that can precipitate a coronary event, so this drug must be tapered. Deprescribing changes one drug at a time with a plan, never everything at once. The principle applies at any age wherever a drug lacks benefit or causes harm. Halving every dose simultaneously is the same abrupt reduction applied across his whole regimen and makes any resulting problem impossible to attribute."}, {"id": 570, "domain": "management", "question": "You have explained a new warfarin prescription to a 70-year-old woman, covering the indication, monitoring, interactions and bleeding precautions. She nods and says she has no questions. Which action best confirms that she can actually manage the medication safely at home?", "options": {"A": "Give her a printed information leaflet and book a follow up appointment", "B": "Ask whether she has any further questions before she leaves the clinic", "C": "Telephone her community pharmacist to repeat the same counselling points", "D": "Ask her to describe in her own words how she will take it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask her to describe in her own words how she will take it): teach back is the method that verifies understanding, because a person can nod and agree while having understood very little. Written material supports recall but does not test comprehension, and reading level or language may make it inaccessible. Asking whether she has questions is the technique teach back exists to replace, since most people say no. Pharmacist reinforcement is valuable and should happen anyway, but delegating verification does not tell you what she understood in the room."}, {"id": 583, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A client with type 2 diabetes says he has stopped checking his glucose because the numbers make him anxious. You immediately begin explaining why monitoring matters and how the readings guide his medication choices. He goes quiet and looks at the floor. Which error have you most likely made?", "options": {"A": "Failure to assess his health literacy before providing the explanation", "B": "Failure to obtain informed consent for a change in his monitoring", "C": "The righting reflex, correcting him with information he did not request", "D": "Premature reassurance, minimizing the anxiety that his readings produce"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The righting reflex, correcting him with information he did not request): the urge to fix, warn and correct is the most common error in this domain and shows up as accurate information delivered to someone who never asked for it. Asking permission before sharing would have preserved the collaboration. Health literacy is not the barrier here, since he understands monitoring well enough to be frightened by it. Nothing in the exchange minimized his anxiety, because the anxiety was never acknowledged at all."}, {"id": 585, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A client who missed her last two appointments arrives on time for this one, in a week during which her housing fell through. You want to offer an affirmation as motivational interviewing defines it, rather than generic praise. Which statement best meets that standard?", "options": {"A": "Well done for coming in today, that is really good to see", "B": "I am proud of you for making the effort to attend", "C": "Attendance matters, and missing appointments makes your care much harder", "D": "You got here in a week that took your housing away"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (You got here in a week that took your housing away): an affirmation names a specific real strength the person demonstrated, tied to her actual circumstances, rather than offering general approval. Generic praise carries no information about what she actually did. Statements built around the clinician's pride centre the clinician and reinforce the power imbalance in the room. Reminding her that missed appointments complicate care is a reproach dressed up as feedback."}, {"id": 598, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A client who has been stable on opioid agonist therapy attended a walk in clinic last week asking for help with her drinking. She now reports four days of sweating, nausea, muscle aches and agitation. Reviewing the pharmacy profile shown, which entry best explains her presentation?", "options": {"A": "Sertraline, through serotonin excess combined with her other daily medications", "B": "Thiamine, which commonly causes gastrointestinal upset at this daily dose", "C": "Ramipril, producing symptomatic hypotension during the first treatment week", "D": "Naltrexone, which precipitates withdrawal in anyone taking an opioid agonist"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Naltrexone, which precipitates withdrawal in anyone taking an opioid agonist): naltrexone is an opioid antagonist and is contraindicated in anyone receiving opioid agonist therapy, because it displaces the agonist and precipitates acute withdrawal. Acamprosate would have been the appropriate first line choice for alcohol use disorder in a person on this regimen. A stable antidepressant dose does not explain an abrupt deterioration of this pattern. Thiamine is well tolerated, and an angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor does not produce sweating, muscle aches and agitation together.", "image": "question_images/q_598.png"}, {"id": 610, "domain": "counselling", "question": "She asks whether she can simply stop at home this coming weekend and get it over with. You are assessing whether an unsupervised community withdrawal would be safe for her. Which feature of the history you have taken weighs most heavily against that plan?", "options": {"A": "The seizure that followed her last abrupt cessation of drinking", "B": "The larger volume she drinks on weekends compared with weekdays", "C": "Living alone without another adult present in the home", "D": "Her stated intention to stop completely rather than reduce gradually"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The seizure that followed her last abrupt cessation of drinking): a past withdrawal seizure is among the strongest predictors of complicated withdrawal and pushes management toward a supervised or inpatient setting. Heavy daily intake and significant comorbidity also add risk, but the weekday to weekend difference in volume is a much weaker signal. Living alone increases the danger if a seizure occurs, yet it is the seizure history itself that establishes the risk in the first place. Choosing abstinence over gradual reduction is a treatment goal, not a risk factor.", "case_id": "C050", "case_text": "A 52-year-old woman joins your primary care roster after losing her family physician.\nShe drinks about a bottle of wine each evening and more on weekends.\nTwo years ago she had a seizure two days after stopping abruptly on her own.\nShe lives alone in stable housing, works full time, takes no medication, and has no other medical conditions.\nShe says she wants to stop drinking completely this time and asks for help.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 611, "domain": "counselling", "question": "She is admitted for supervised withdrawal management. Her last drink was eighteen hours ago, she is tremulous, she has vomited twice, she eats poorly, and intravenous fluids containing dextrose are being prepared. Which order is most important to complete before those fluids are started?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a serum ethanol level to confirm the timing of her last drink", "B": "Start a long acting benzodiazepine on a symptom triggered dosing protocol", "C": "Give oral magnesium and a multivitamin along with the first fluid bag", "D": "Give parenteral thiamine before any glucose containing fluid is infused"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give parenteral thiamine before any glucose containing fluid is infused): glucose given to a thiamine depleted person can precipitate Wernicke encephalopathy, so thiamine goes first and by a parenteral route when absorption cannot be relied on. Benzodiazepines are the correct treatment for the withdrawal syndrome itself and will be needed, but they do not carry this time critical sequencing. A serum ethanol level does not change immediate management in someone who is already symptomatic. Oral supplements are unreliable in a person who has just vomited twice.", "case_id": "C050", "case_text": "A 52-year-old woman joins your primary care roster after losing her family physician.\nShe drinks about a bottle of wine each evening and more on weekends.\nTwo years ago she had a seizure two days after stopping abruptly on her own.\nShe lives alone in stable housing, works full time, takes no medication, and has no other medical conditions.\nShe says she wants to stop drinking completely this time and asks for help.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 612, "domain": "counselling", "question": "She completes withdrawal management without complication and asks about medication to help her stay stopped. She takes no opioids, her estimated glomerular filtration rate is normal, and she has no evidence of liver decompensation. Which first line option should you discuss with her at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Disulfiram, which produces an aversive reaction if she drinks alcohol", "B": "Gabapentin, prescribed off label to reduce the intensity of craving", "C": "Naltrexone, which reduces heavy drinking and suits her clinical situation", "D": "Long term benzodiazepine therapy to prevent recurrent withdrawal symptoms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Naltrexone, which reduces heavy drinking and suits her clinical situation): naltrexone and acamprosate are the first line agents for alcohol use disorder, and naltrexone fits here because she takes no opioids and has no contraindication to it. Acamprosate would also be reasonable and is renally cleared, which her normal filtration rate would allow. Disulfiram is a later option that requires supervision and full informed consent. Continuing benzodiazepines beyond the withdrawal period creates dependence and does nothing to treat the underlying disorder.", "case_id": "C050", "case_text": "A 52-year-old woman joins your primary care roster after losing her family physician.\nShe drinks about a bottle of wine each evening and more on weekends.\nTwo years ago she had a seizure two days after stopping abruptly on her own.\nShe lives alone in stable housing, works full time, takes no medication, and has no other medical conditions.\nShe says she wants to stop drinking completely this time and asks for help.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 613, "domain": "counselling", "question": "Three months later she reports two heavy drinking days after a family funeral, then resumed her medication and has not drunk since. She is worried that you will stop treating her and asks whether her treatment has now failed. Which response best supports her ongoing care?", "options": {"A": "Treat the episode as expected, review the triggers and continue the medication", "B": "Restart the full treatment pathway including a repeat withdrawal risk assessment", "C": "Discontinue the naltrexone, since further drinking shows it is not working", "D": "Refer her to residential treatment as the logical next step in intensity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat the episode as expected, review the triggers and continue the medication): return to use is a normal event in the cycle of change, and the clinical task is identifying the high risk situation and rehearsing a response while keeping effective treatment in place. A repeat withdrawal assessment is not indicated when she is not in withdrawal and has already resumed abstinence. Stopping the medication after a single episode removes support at the point of greatest vulnerability. Escalating to residential treatment ignores that she recovered the plan herself within days.", "case_id": "C050", "case_text": "A 52-year-old woman joins your primary care roster after losing her family physician.\nShe drinks about a bottle of wine each evening and more on weekends.\nTwo years ago she had a seizure two days after stopping abruptly on her own.\nShe lives alone in stable housing, works full time, takes no medication, and has no other medical conditions.\nShe says she wants to stop drinking completely this time and asks for help.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 614, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A client tells you he once left a friend alone during an overdose because he had drugs in his pocket and feared being arrested. He asks what would have happened to him if he had called for an ambulance. Which statement about Canadian law should you give him?", "options": {"A": "Calling for help grants complete immunity from all charges arising at the scene", "B": "The Good Samaritan Drug Overdose Act protects callers from simple possession charges", "C": "Protection applies only if the caller remains at the scene until police arrive", "D": "Provincial law determines whether a caller can be charged with drug possession"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Good Samaritan Drug Overdose Act protects callers from simple possession charges): the federal Good Samaritan Drug Overdose Act shields the person who calls for help, and others present at the scene, from charges for simple possession of a controlled substance. The protection is not blanket immunity, since outstanding warrants and offences such as trafficking are not covered by it. Nothing in the Act conditions the protection on waiting for police to arrive. Criminal law is federal, so a provincial rule does not decide whether possession charges apply."}, {"id": 617, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A client who uses alone in her apartment tells you she now uses fentanyl test strips and treats a negative result as proof that a sample is safe. She asks you to confirm that she is right about this. Which response is the most accurate and most useful counselling?", "options": {"A": "Confirm that a negative strip reliably excludes fentanyl from that entire drug sample", "B": "Advise her that drug checking is unreliable and therefore not worth using", "C": "Explain that uneven mixing means a negative strip cannot make a sample safe", "D": "Recommend she switch to spectrometry, which detects every possible adulterant"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that uneven mixing means a negative strip cannot make a sample safe): substances are not evenly distributed through a sample, so a strip testing one portion can easily miss fentanyl present elsewhere in the same supply. Drug checking still has real value in informing decisions, so telling her it is not worth using discards a useful harm reduction tool. Spectrometry gives richer information than a strip but does not detect everything and cannot make any sample safe. Pairing the correction with not using alone addresses the risk she is actually carrying."}, {"id": 618, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A man with severe alcohol use disorder and no fixed address is repeatedly brought to the emergency department after drinking hand sanitizer. He declines abstinence based treatment. A community agency asks whether a managed alcohol programme would help him. Which outcome is best supported by the evidence?", "options": {"A": "Reduced non beverage alcohol consumption and fewer emergency service contacts", "B": "Higher rates of long term abstinence than standard residential treatment achieves", "C": "Complete elimination of alcohol related liver disease within the first year", "D": "Increased overall alcohol consumption offset by much improved housing stability"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced non beverage alcohol consumption and fewer emergency service contacts): managed alcohol programmes supply measured regular doses of beverage alcohol and are associated with less use of non beverage alcohol such as hand sanitizer, together with reduced emergency service use. The model is not designed to produce abstinence and is not compared favourably with residential treatment on that outcome. Organ damage already present is not eliminated by stabilizing a pattern of drinking. Participants typically drink less overall rather than more, since consumption becomes measured and scheduled."}, {"id": 619, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A colleague documents that a client admitted to abusing opioids, was dirty on today's screen, but has been clean for two months. You are revising the clinic's charting guidance and want to show the team a better version. Which rewording best reflects non stigmatizing person first language?", "options": {"A": "Client denies opioid abuse today and reports two months of continuous sobriety", "B": "Client reported opioid use and had a positive urine drug test today", "C": "Known opioid user, urine positive today, sober for the past two months", "D": "Client relapsed after two clean months, urine screen dirty on review today"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Client reported opioid use and had a positive urine drug test today): person first wording describes the behaviour and the test result without moral framing, using positive rather than dirty and use rather than abuse. Describing what a client denies imports the language of accusation into the permanent record. Labelling someone a known user attaches an identity to the person instead of describing a behaviour. Calling a return to use a relapse and a result dirty preserves the exact terms shown to shift clinicians toward more punitive decisions."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A client stabilized on opioid agonist therapy smokes twenty cigarettes daily and asks about quitting. A team member argues that addressing tobacco now risks destabilizing his opioid treatment and should wait a year. He has no psychiatric history. Which response best reflects the evidence?", "options": {"A": "Defer cessation for one year in order to protect his treatment stability", "B": "Offer cessation now, combining pharmacotherapy with structured behavioural support", "C": "Offer behavioural support alone, since cessation medications interact with his therapy", "D": "Advise gradual reduction in cigarettes rather than a formal quit attempt"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Offer cessation now, combining pharmacotherapy with structured behavioural support): tobacco causes much of the long term mortality in this population, treating it does not destabilize opioid agonist therapy, and medication combined with behavioural support outperforms either component alone. Varenicline, combination nicotine replacement pairing a patch with a short acting form, and bupropion are all reasonable choices to discuss. Delaying a year loses the moment when he has actually asked, which is the strongest predictor of an attempt. Counselling alone and unstructured gradual reduction both produce lower abstinence rates."}, {"id": 629, "domain": "counselling", "question": "A person known to the clinic for opioid use disorder arrives drowsy, slurring his words and smelling of alcohol, and front desk staff assume he is intoxicated and ask him to wait. His capillary glucose is 2.8 mmol/L. Which lesson should the team take from this event?", "options": {"A": "Presentations must be assessed before intoxication is assumed, using standardized triage", "B": "Front desk staff should not perform any clinical assessment of arriving clients", "C": "Clients with a substance use disorder require capillary glucose at every visit", "D": "The error reflects individual judgement rather than a system level problem"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Presentations must be assessed before intoxication is assumed, using standardized triage): treating a presentation as intoxication before assessment is the documented pathway to missed diagnoses, and the countermeasure is a standardized process applied to everyone rather than personal vigilance. British Columbia's In Plain Sight review described exactly this pattern and its consequences. Removing front desk observation entirely discards useful information instead of structuring how it is used. Framing the event as one person's poor judgement protects the system that allowed an assumption to determine care."}, {"id": 645, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "It is now Monday at 09:15 and nothing has been done since Friday. You have a full clinic and your assistant is asking whether she should call the client or the privacy commissioner first. Which action takes priority over the others at this moment?", "options": {"A": "Notify the client that her health information was sent to the wrong place", "B": "Report the incident to the provincial privacy commissioner and your college", "C": "Begin a root cause review of how the fax number was misdialled", "D": "Telephone the construction company and arrange retrieval of the faxed package"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Telephone the construction company and arrange retrieval of the faxed package): breach response runs contain, notify, investigate, prevent, and containment comes first because every hour the package sits in an unsecured office widens the exposure. Notifying the client matters greatly and follows immediately, but it does nothing to limit the disclosure already in progress. Reporting externally and analysing the cause both belong to later steps and cannot be completed properly until the scope is known. Retrieving or securing the information is the only action that changes how much harm is still possible.", "case_id": "C052", "case_text": "You are a self employed nurse practitioner running a small primary care practice in Saskatchewan. On Monday your administrative assistant tells you that a referral package for one client, including a consultation note and recent laboratory results, was faxed on Friday to a construction company rather than to the specialist's office. The receiving number differs from the correct one by a single digit. The client has not been told.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 646, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "Your assistant sent the fax and asks whether she or you will be held responsible under the provincial health privacy statute. You are the sole owner of the practice and she works for you. Which statement most accurately describes accountability here?", "options": {"A": "Accountability rests with the assistant, since she personally misdirected the referral fax", "B": "You are the trustee and remain accountable for the acts of your employees", "C": "Accountability is shared equally, because both of you handled the client's information", "D": "The specialist becomes accountable once the referral package leaves your practice entirely"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (You are the trustee and remain accountable for the acts of your employees): a self employed nurse practitioner personally holds custody and control of the health information in the practice, so safeguards, training, breach response and access requests all land on the owner. Staff who handle records act for the trustee rather than as independent trustees in their own right. Nothing transfers accountability to a specialist who never received the package. Employees can face their own consequences, but that does not divide or dilute the trustee's statutory duty.", "case_id": "C052", "case_text": "You are a self employed nurse practitioner running a small primary care practice in Saskatchewan. On Monday your administrative assistant tells you that a referral package for one client, including a consultation note and recent laboratory results, was faxed on Friday to a construction company rather than to the specialist's office. The receiving number differs from the correct one by a single digit. The client has not been told.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 647, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "The package is retrieved and shredded by the company that received it. You now telephone the client, who is upset and asks what happens now. Beyond an apology, which combination of information does she have the strongest claim to receive from you during this conversation?", "options": {"A": "What was disclosed, what you have done, and how to complain externally", "B": "The name of the staff member responsible and the discipline she received", "C": "Your legal opinion on whether the disclosure meets the harm reporting threshold", "D": "A written assurance that no further breach will occur in your practice"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (What was disclosed, what you have done, and how to complain externally): notification exists so the affected person can judge her own risk and pursue an independent remedy, which requires knowing the content involved, the containment steps taken, and the route to the privacy commissioner. Identifying an employee and describing internal discipline is her employer's private business and adds nothing to the client's ability to protect herself. Offering a legal conclusion about reporting thresholds gives her nothing she can act on, and assessing the risk of harm is a judgment the trustee must make and document rather than a verdict to deliver on the telephone. Promising that no breach will ever recur is an assurance no practice can honestly give.", "case_id": "C052", "case_text": "You are a self employed nurse practitioner running a small primary care practice in Saskatchewan. On Monday your administrative assistant tells you that a referral package for one client, including a consultation note and recent laboratory results, was faxed on Friday to a construction company rather than to the specialist's office. The receiving number differs from the correct one by a single digit. The client has not been told.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 648, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "Three weeks later you are documenting your prevention steps. Your practice sends roughly forty referral faxes each week and has no formal process. Which change most directly removes the failure mode that caused this breach, rather than only reducing its consequences after it happens again?", "options": {"A": "Add a confidentiality notice to the cover page of every outgoing fax", "B": "Retrain all staff annually on privacy obligations and breach reporting duties", "C": "Send referrals through the encrypted provincial system using stored verified numbers", "D": "Require a second staff member to witness every fax before transmission"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Send referrals through the encrypted provincial system using stored verified numbers): the failure was manual entry of a destination address, so eliminating hand keyed numbers and moving to encrypted transmission removes the hazard rather than managing it. A confidentiality notice asks a stranger to behave well after the information has already arrived. Annual retraining does not change the moment at which a digit is mistyped by a busy person. A witness step adds a second human to a task that humans reliably get wrong and degrades as soon as the clinic is short staffed.", "case_id": "C052", "case_text": "You are a self employed nurse practitioner running a small primary care practice in Saskatchewan. On Monday your administrative assistant tells you that a referral package for one client, including a consultation note and recent laboratory results, was faxed on Friday to a construction company rather than to the specialist's office. The receiving number differs from the correct one by a single digit. The client has not been told.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 661, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "You are handing over an unstable client to the on call nurse practitioner at shift change. You have described the situation, the background and your assessment. The on call colleague says only, understood, thanks. Why is transfer of accountability not yet complete?", "options": {"A": "The handover was verbal and has not yet been entered into the chart", "B": "No recommendation was stated and the colleague has not accepted the next step", "C": "The client and family were not present to hear the handover discussion", "D": "A structured written handover tool was not used alongside the verbal report"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No recommendation was stated and the colleague has not accepted the next step): transfer of accountability is complete only when the receiver holds the information, has had the chance to ask questions, and has accepted responsibility for a specific action. Ending at assessment leaves the next step unowned no matter how accurate the description was. A verbal handover is normal practice and charting it afterwards does not create the missing acceptance. Family presence is not a component of clinician to clinician handover, and a written tool without a stated ask has the same defect."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "A First Nations child living on reserve needs an urgent speech language assessment before school starts. The provincial program has a nine month wait and the band office says funding responsibility is unclear. Which action most directly addresses the funding barrier for this child?", "options": {"A": "Submit a Jordan's Principle request so the assessment proceeds without jurisdictional delay", "B": "Refer to the provincial program and document the wait time in the chart", "C": "Apply to the provincial drug benefit program for coverage of the assessment", "D": "Advise the family to seek private assessment and claim it on taxes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Submit a Jordan's Principle request so the assessment proceeds without jurisdictional delay): Jordan's Principle exists precisely so that a First Nations child receives the service first and governments settle the question of who pays afterwards. Referring into a nine month queue and recording the wait documents the barrier without removing it. A drug benefit program funds medications and has no mechanism for an assessment of this kind. Advising a family to purchase the service privately shifts a public obligation onto them and ignores the funding route that was created for this situation."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "A client moves from your surgical unit to the medicine unit on day three. Her anticoagulant was held for surgery on day one. Both units use the same electronic record and the discharge reconciliation is scheduled for day six. What is the greatest risk here?", "options": {"A": "The medicine team may duplicate the anticoagulant order already entered on day one", "B": "The shared record may not display the surgical indication for holding the drug", "C": "The held anticoagulant stays invisible until someone compares lists at this transfer", "D": "The discharge reconciliation may rely on an outdated community pharmacy profile"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The held anticoagulant stays invisible until someone compares lists at this transfer): reconciliation is required at admission, at every internal transfer and at discharge, and skipping the transfer step is what allows a drug held for a procedure to be quietly never restarted. A held order does not present itself to the receiving team the way an active order does, which is exactly why the comparison has to be deliberate. A duplicate order would at least be visible on the administration record. Sharing one electronic system creates a false sense that the list has already been checked."}, {"id": 667, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "A client with unstable bipolar disorder is moving to another province in three weeks. She has a lithium level due monthly and no receiving provider yet. She asks you to keep prescribing remotely until she finds someone. What is your best response?", "options": {"A": "Agree, since continuity of lithium therapy outweighs the licensing considerations involved", "B": "Decline, and advise her to attend a walk in clinic after she moves", "C": "Agree for three months, then transfer her care to a new provider", "D": "Decline remote prescribing, and actively arrange a receiving provider before she moves"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline remote prescribing, and actively arrange a receiving provider before she moves): registration follows the client's physical location during the encounter, so prescribing into another province without licensure there is not available to you, and leading a transition means securing the receiving clinician rather than leaving the client to find one. Continuity is a reason to work harder on the referral, not a reason to practise unregistered. Sending a woman with unstable bipolar disorder and monthly lithium monitoring to a walk in clinic guarantees fragmented care. A three month arrangement has the same licensing defect and simply postpones it."}, {"id": 674, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "You assess a 45 year old with three days of low back pain, no red flags, and normal neurological findings. He requests magnetic resonance imaging and you decline. Two years later this decision is reviewed. Which entry best defends your decision?", "options": {"A": "Red flags screened and absent, imaging not indicated, rationale discussed, review planned", "B": "Magnetic resonance imaging declined per Choosing Wisely Canada recommendation for back pain", "C": "Client requested imaging, advised it was unnecessary, given exercise advice and analgesia", "D": "Acute mechanical low back pain, conservative management, imaging deferred at this time"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Red flags screened and absent, imaging not indicated, rationale discussed, review planned): rationale matters most for decisions that can look wrong in hindsight, and this entry records the screening performed, the conclusion drawn from it, the discussion held and the safety net set. Citing a national recommendation states an authority without showing that this client was assessed against it. Recording that imaging was declined and advice given documents actions without the reasoning that connects findings to decision. A diagnostic label with conservative management describes the plan but leaves a reviewer unable to see what you ruled out."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "A routine audit shows a nurse practitioner on your team opened the chart of a neighbour who was admitted overnight. She was not involved in that admission and says she only wanted to know whether he was all right. How should this be characterized?", "options": {"A": "An error of judgment, but not a privacy breach since nothing was disclosed", "B": "A privacy breach, because access without a care purpose is unauthorized use", "C": "Acceptable, because she is a regulated clinician with legitimate system credentials", "D": "A breach only if she repeats the access or discusses the contents with others"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A privacy breach, because access without a care purpose is unauthorized use): a breach includes any unauthorized collection, use, disclosure, loss or theft of personal health information, and looking at a record when you are not part of that person's care is unauthorized use whether or not anything is repeated. This behaviour, commonly called snooping, is the most frequent privacy offence committed by Canadian health professionals. Valid credentials grant technical access to a system, not permission to read any chart in it. Concern for a neighbour explains the motive without changing the classification."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "transition_of_care_and_documentation", "question": "You practise in Nova Scotia. A long standing client telephones for a medication adjustment that is clinically straightforward, and mentions she is currently staying with her sister in British Columbia for three months. What is the most important consideration before proceeding?", "options": {"A": "Licensure follows the client's physical location, so British Columbia registration may apply", "B": "Your Nova Scotia registration governs, because the therapeutic relationship began there", "C": "Registration is irrelevant for telephone advice not involving a physical examination", "D": "Federal mobility provisions allow any Canadian licence to be used across provinces"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Licensure follows the client's physical location, so British Columbia registration may apply): you must be licensed where the client is physically located during the encounter rather than where you happen to be sitting, and a three month stay in another province brings that jurisdiction's regulator into the picture. The origin of the therapeutic relationship does not extend your registration across a provincial boundary. The absence of a physical examination changes the assessment, not the requirement to be registered. Labour mobility supports registration in another province through an application process rather than making one licence valid everywhere automatically."}, {"id": 713, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "An NP wants to search the literature after wondering whether exercise helps her patients living with depression. Her first attempt, does exercise help depression, returns thousands of irrelevant results and no usable trials. Which reformulation of the question will make her search both specific and answerable?", "options": {"A": "Does exercise improve mood in adults with depression seen in primary care", "B": "What is the best non-pharmacological option for adults living with depression", "C": "Is supervised exercise or antidepressant medication superior for mental health in all adults", "D": "In adults with mild depression, does supervised aerobic exercise reduce 12 week symptoms"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (In adults with mild depression, does supervised aerobic exercise reduce 12 week symptoms): this version names the population, the intervention, the implied comparison of usual care, the outcome and the timeframe, which is what makes a search strategy reproducible and its results relevant. Asking whether exercise improves mood leaves both intervention and outcome undefined, so nothing can be narrowed. Asking for the single best option requests a conclusion rather than a searchable question. Framing the comparison across all adults removes the population boundary that keeps retrieved studies applicable."}, {"id": 718, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "A trial reports that participants were randomized using a computer generated sequence held in a sealed central registry, and that neither the recruiter nor the participant could see the next assignment before enrolment. Which specific threat to validity does this arrangement address?", "options": {"A": "Attrition bias, by keeping participants engaged until the final study visit", "B": "Measurement bias, by keeping the outcome assessor unaware of group assignment", "C": "Publication bias, by registering the trial before any recruitment begins", "D": "Selection bias, by preventing recruiters from steering patients into an arm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Selection bias, by preventing recruiters from steering patients into an arm): allocation concealment hides the upcoming assignment at the moment of enrolment, so a recruiter who believes in the intervention cannot delay or divert a sicker patient into the comparator. Randomization only balances groups when nobody involved can see or predict what comes next. Keeping the outcome assessor unaware is blinding, a separate safeguard operating after allocation. Prospective registration and retention are both worthwhile, but neither is what a concealed sequence accomplishes."}, {"id": 721, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "In a trial of an anticoagulant, major bleeding occurred in 2.5% of treated participants and 0.5% of those receiving placebo, while the number needed to treat to prevent one stroke was 60. What is the number needed to harm here, and what does that comparison suggest?", "options": {"A": "50, so roughly one bleed for every 50 treated, against one stroke per 60", "B": "20, so roughly one bleed for every 20 treated, against one stroke per 60", "C": "200, so roughly one bleed for every 200 treated, against one stroke per 60", "D": "5, so roughly one bleed for every 5 treated, against one stroke per 60"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (50, so roughly one bleed for every 50 treated, against one stroke per 60): the absolute risk increase is 2.5% minus 0.5%, which is 2 percentage points or 0.02, and 1 ÷ 0.02 = 50. Because harm arrives slightly more often than benefit on these figures, the trade needs an explicit conversation about which outcome the patient fears more. Taking the reciprocal of either single event rate answers a different question, since neither one is a difference between groups. The fivefold relative increase in bleeding sounds alarming but describes the very same 2 percentage point gap."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "An NP reads an interpretive description study of why South Asian women in her city decline cardiac rehabilitation. A colleague dismisses the paper because only 14 women were interviewed. What is the most appropriate basis for judging the quality of a study of this kind?", "options": {"A": "Credibility, transferability, dependability and confirmability of the interpretive account", "B": "Statistical power, since a sample of 14 cannot support any conclusion", "C": "Randomization of participants, which prevents systematic bias in selection", "D": "The confidence interval around the proportion of women declining rehabilitation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Credibility, transferability, dependability and confirmability of the interpretive account): qualitative work is judged on whether the interpretation fits the data, whether context is described richly enough for a reader to judge transfer, whether the process is auditable and whether findings arise from participants rather than the researcher. Sample size in this tradition is governed by depth and information power, not by a power calculation. Randomization and confidence intervals belong to designs that estimate effects in a population. Applying quantitative yardsticks to a question about meaning is a category error rather than rigour."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "The graph shown summarizes a trial of a text message reminder programme in 9,400 adults living with hypertension. The between group difference at 12 months was reported as statistically significant, with p = 0.004. What is the most defensible interpretation for practice?", "options": {"A": "The programme should be adopted widely, since the difference is statistically significant", "B": "The programme failed entirely, because both arms remained above the treatment target", "C": "A 2 mmHg difference is real but too small to justify the programme alone", "D": "The p value shows a 99.6% chance the programme lowers blood pressure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A 2 mmHg difference is real but too small to justify the programme alone): with 9,400 participants a trial can detect differences far below the size that changes outcomes for any individual, and a p value of 0.004 attached to a 2 mmHg gap is exactly that situation. Statistical significance answers whether a difference is likely to be real, never whether it is large enough to matter. A p value is also not the probability that the programme works, since it is calculated assuming no true difference exists. Both arms sitting above the systolic target argues for intensifying therapy rather than for calling a small real effect nonexistent.", "image": "question_images/q_727.png"}, {"id": 736, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "A research team invites two NPs, a client partner and a clinic manager to help shape the research question, choose the outcomes and plan the study, all before any funding application is submitted. Which term best describes this approach, and why does it matter?", "options": {"A": "End of grant dissemination, which improves the reach of published findings", "B": "Program evaluation, which measures whether an existing service met its objectives", "C": "Peer review, which improves methodological quality before a study is funded", "D": "Integrated knowledge translation, which raises the odds findings will be usable"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Integrated knowledge translation, which raises the odds findings will be usable): involving knowledge users from the moment the question is framed is what defines this approach, and it makes the chosen outcomes the ones decision makers actually need. Activity that begins once results exist, such as publication and conference presentation, arrives far too late to shape the study. Program evaluation assesses a service already running rather than designing new inquiry. Peer review is an external check on method, not a partnership with the people who will use the results."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "A colleague tells the NP that this project is quality improvement and therefore exempt from research ethics board review under TCPS 2. Considering the stated intent of the project and the way participants will be allocated, which assessment of that advice is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is exempt, since the group visit model is already used in other clinics", "B": "It is exempt, since the data comes from her own rostered clients only", "C": "Review is required, because the project seeks new knowledge beyond local improvement", "D": "Review is required only if the results are eventually submitted for publication"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review is required, because the project seeks new knowledge beyond local improvement): TCPS 2 defines research as an undertaking intended to extend knowledge through a disciplined inquiry or systematic investigation, and its Article 2.5 exclusion reaches only work used exclusively for local assessment, management or improvement, which testing an unproven model to find out whether it works is not. Intent at the outset determines the pathway, so this project needs board review before recruitment begins. Whether the model is used elsewhere does not change what is being done to these particular participants. Deciding to publish afterwards cannot retroactively supply the oversight and consent that should have been in place from the start.", "case_id": "C061", "case_text": "An NP in a northern Ontario community wants to test whether a monthly group visit model improves glycemic control for adults with type 2 diabetes.\nShe plans to allocate 90 of her own rostered clients by chance to group visits or to usual individual appointments, measure A1c at 12 months, and publish the results.\nRoughly one third of her roster are members of a nearby First Nations community.\nShe intends to recruit clients herself during their regular appointments.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 750, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "The NP plans to invite each client into the study herself during a routine appointment, immediately after reviewing their blood work and adjusting their medications. What is the single most important modification to make to this recruitment plan before the project begins?", "options": {"A": "Provide a written information sheet and obtain a signature at that same visit", "B": "Offer a modest gift card so that participation feels fairly compensated", "C": "Recruit only clients whose A1c is above target, to strengthen the signal", "D": "Have an independent person seek consent, since the dual role creates pressure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Have an independent person seek consent, since the dual role creates pressure): a client whose ongoing care depends on this NP may feel unable to decline an invitation that arrives from her, in her office, in the middle of a treatment discussion. Separating the consent conversation from the therapeutic relationship protects the voluntariness that free and informed consent requires. A signature documents consent but does nothing about the pressure that produced it. Payment can shade into undue inducement, and selecting participants by A1c changes the science rather than the ethics of recruitment.", "case_id": "C061", "case_text": "An NP in a northern Ontario community wants to test whether a monthly group visit model improves glycemic control for adults with type 2 diabetes.\nShe plans to allocate 90 of her own rostered clients by chance to group visits or to usual individual appointments, measure A1c at 12 months, and publish the results.\nRoughly one third of her roster are members of a nearby First Nations community.\nShe intends to recruit clients herself during their regular appointments.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 751, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "One third of the roster are members of a nearby First Nations community, and the analysis will report results by Indigenous identity. Applying Chapter 9 of TCPS 2 to this proposal, at what point and in what way should that community be involved in the project?", "options": {"A": "After the results are analysed, by presenting findings at a community open house", "B": "Before the protocol is finalized, through engagement about design, benefit and data use", "C": "Only if individual First Nations participants ask for community level consultation first", "D": "At the consent stage, by adding a question about Indigenous identity to forms"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Before the protocol is finalized, through engagement about design, benefit and data use): Chapter 9 expects engagement proportional to how far the research reaches into collective interests, and reporting results by Indigenous identity clearly does, so the conversation belongs at the design stage. Engagement covers the question being asked, how data will be governed, what the community gains and how findings will be reported. Presenting completed results is dissemination rather than engagement, and it arrives after every decision has already been made. Collective interests are not something an individual participant can waive for a community.", "case_id": "C061", "case_text": "An NP in a northern Ontario community wants to test whether a monthly group visit model improves glycemic control for adults with type 2 diabetes.\nShe plans to allocate 90 of her own rostered clients by chance to group visits or to usual individual appointments, measure A1c at 12 months, and publish the results.\nRoughly one third of her roster are members of a nearby First Nations community.\nShe intends to recruit clients herself during their regular appointments.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 752, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "During engagement, the First Nations community asks how data about its members will be governed once the study has ended and the analysis is published. The NP wants to answer in a way consistent with OCAP. Which commitment reflects those four principles most accurately?", "options": {"A": "The community owns and controls its data and decides on access and stewardship", "B": "The university sponsor holds the data and grants the community reading access", "C": "OCAP applies equally to all Indigenous Peoples, so one agreement covers everyone", "D": "Deidentifying the records removes any further community interest in the data"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The community owns and controls its data and decides on access and stewardship): ownership means the community collectively owns information about its members, control means it directs every stage of research affecting it, access means it can reach that information wherever it is held, and possession is physical stewardship of the data itself. Placing the sponsor in charge and granting reading rights inverts the relationship these principles describe. The principles are asserted by First Nations, while Inuit and Métis Nations articulate their own governance approaches, so a single blanket agreement is not appropriate. Removing identifiers does not extinguish a collective interest in community data.", "case_id": "C061", "case_text": "An NP in a northern Ontario community wants to test whether a monthly group visit model improves glycemic control for adults with type 2 diabetes.\nShe plans to allocate 90 of her own rostered clients by chance to group visits or to usual individual appointments, measure A1c at 12 months, and publish the results.\nRoughly one third of her roster are members of a nearby First Nations community.\nShe intends to recruit clients herself during their regular appointments.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 753, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "The run chart shown plots a monthly quality measure in time order, and the median of all 12 points is 58%. A deliberate change to the admission process was introduced after month 6. Which conclusion about this chart is best supported by the pattern shown?", "options": {"A": "Common cause variation only, since every point falls within the usual range", "B": "A trend, since five or more consecutive points rise steadily across the chart", "C": "A shift, since six consecutive points fall above the median after the change", "D": "An astronomical point in month 12, warranting investigation of that month alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A shift, since six consecutive points fall above the median after the change): the final six months all sit above the median of 58%, and six or more consecutive points on one side of the median is a recognized signal that something other than chance is at work. Arriving immediately after a change the team introduced on purpose, that is evidence the change did something. A trend requires five or more consecutive points each higher than the one before, and these later points rise and fall rather than climbing steadily. Randomness would scatter points on both sides, and no single value sits far outside the rest.", "image": "question_images/q_753.png"}, {"id": 767, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "After a delayed cancer diagnosis, a team reconstructs the timeline, repeatedly asks why each step failed, and maps contributing factors across people, process, equipment and environment. Which feature of the finished report will best distinguish a strong analysis from a weak one?", "options": {"A": "Recommendations that change the system, each with a named owner and date", "B": "A complete list of every individual who touched the client's care pathway", "C": "A conclusion identifying which single clinician made the critical diagnostic error", "D": "A recommendation that all staff complete additional diagnostic reasoning training"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recommendations that change the system, each with a named owner and date): an analysis is only useful when it produces changes to the conditions of work, and an owner with a deadline is what turns a recommendation into something that actually happens. Cataloguing who was present, or naming a single person to blame, ends the inquiry precisely where the real learning begins. Blanket training is the classic weak recommendation, because it asks people to be more careful inside a system that has not changed at all. Strength is judged by how much a recommendation reduces reliance on individual vigilance."}, {"id": 772, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "An NP is drafting the data governance section for a study involving Inuit participants in Nunavut, and plans to state that the project will follow OCAP principles throughout. A reviewer flags this wording as inaccurate. What is the correct explanation for that flag?", "options": {"A": "OCAP applies to all research in Canada involving any identifiable community group", "B": "OCAP has been replaced by Chapter 9 of TCPS 2 for all Indigenous research", "C": "OCAP is a First Nations framework, and Inuit assert their own governance approaches", "D": "OCAP applies only when a First Nations community holds the research funding directly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (OCAP is a First Nations framework, and Inuit assert their own governance approaches): the principles were created by First Nations in 1998 and are stewarded by the First Nations Information Governance Centre, which holds OCAP as a registered trademark, so applying the label to Inuit or Métis participants misrepresents whose framework is being followed. Inuit self determination in research is articulated through Inuit Tapiriit Kanatami's national strategy, and Métis Nations set out their own expectations separately. Chapter 9 of TCPS 2 sets ethics requirements for research involving First Nations, Inuit and Métis Peoples and sits alongside these frameworks rather than replacing them. Who holds the funding does not determine which governance framework applies."}, {"id": 775, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "Six months after a new anticoagulation protocol was introduced with training and a chart template, uptake varies widely: two prescribers follow it consistently and three rarely do, although all five say they support it. Which intervention is now most appropriate for this team?", "options": {"A": "Audit and feedback comparing each prescriber's own rate against the group", "B": "Repeat the original training session for the entire prescribing group again", "C": "Remove the chart template, since it clearly failed to change any behaviour", "D": "Send the protocol document by email to all five prescribers once more"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Audit and feedback comparing each prescriber's own rate against the group): the barrier is neither awareness nor tooling, since everyone endorses the protocol and the template already exists, which leaves unrecognized variation in individual practice. Confidential feedback showing a clinician their own rate beside their peers is the intervention designed for exactly that gap. Repeating training addresses knowledge the group demonstrably already has. Removing the template or resending the document would take away a facilitator or repeat a message, neither of which touches the variation described."}, {"id": 777, "domain": "quality_improvement_and_research", "question": "A control chart of monthly surgical site infection rates shows all points inside limits set at three standard deviations from the mean, except one point sitting well above the upper limit in August. What does that single point most likely represent for this team?", "options": {"A": "Ordinary variation that will regress toward the mean by the following month", "B": "Special cause variation with an assignable reason worth investigating specifically", "C": "Evidence that the control limits were calculated with too few data points", "D": "A shift in the process, since a point outside the limits defines a shift"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Special cause variation with an assignable reason worth investigating specifically): a point beyond three standard deviations is very unlikely to arise from the routine noise of a stable process, so something specific happened that month and finding it is the entire purpose of the chart. That is exactly the situation where investigation is warranted, unlike the ordinary wobble inside the limits. Dismissing it as regression to the mean discards the one signal the chart was built to detect. A shift is defined by a run of consecutive points on one side of the centre line, which is a different signal altogether."}, {"id": 781, "domain": "leader", "question": "The clinic manager asks you to summarize what is actually going wrong before she brings it to the leadership committee. She wants your professional judgment rather than a list of complaints from staff. Which statement best identifies the underlying problem driving the conflicting advice and the patient confusion?", "options": {"A": "A personality conflict between two clinicians that formal mediation should resolve", "B": "Role ambiguity about who adjusts antihypertensive doses for which patients", "C": "A scope of practice breach by the pharmacist requiring college notification", "D": "A charting failure that duplicated medication entries in the shared record"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Role ambiguity about who adjusts antihypertensive doses for which patients): overlapping scope becomes conflict only when nobody has stated out loud who owns which decisions. Both clinicians are practising within scope, so no regulatory breach has occurred and a college notification would be unfounded. Naming it a personality clash hides the structural cause, and mediation would leave the same ambiguity in place. The shared record captured both changes accurately, so the problem is not a charting failure.", "case_id": "C062", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner at a family health team in a mid sized Ontario city.\nOver three months, several patients with hypertension have received conflicting dose advice from you and from the team pharmacist, who also adjusts antihypertensives.\nTwo patients stopped their medication because of the confusion.\nStaff describe the situation as a personality clash between the two of you.\nBoth of you are working within your legislated scope, and neither has raised the issue directly with the other.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 782, "domain": "leader", "question": "A colleague argues that the two of you simply do not get along, and that moving one of you to a different site would settle things. You disagree. Which explanation best accounts for why capable professionals working in good faith each concluded the other was overstepping?", "options": {"A": "Their professional training programs emphasize different approaches to hypertension dose management", "B": "Canadian hypertension guidelines are genuinely ambiguous about who should titrate therapy", "C": "Overlapping competence with no written agreement makes each read the other as intruding", "D": "No shared electronic record exists to show medication changes made by either"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Overlapping competence with no written agreement makes each read the other as intruding): shared competencies let teams cover for each other, but with nothing written down each clinician sees the other acting on their patients. Differences in training do not by themselves generate conflict when roles are clear. Canadian hypertension guidance is not ambiguous about titration, only silent about which profession does it in a given clinic. A shared record does exist and captured both changes, so information availability was not the barrier.", "case_id": "C062", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner at a family health team in a mid sized Ontario city.\nOver three months, several patients with hypertension have received conflicting dose advice from you and from the team pharmacist, who also adjusts antihypertensives.\nTwo patients stopped their medication because of the confusion.\nStaff describe the situation as a personality clash between the two of you.\nBoth of you are working within your legislated scope, and neither has raised the issue directly with the other.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 783, "domain": "leader", "question": "Your assessment is accepted by the team. You now have to choose a first action that will stop patients from receiving contradictory dose advice while preserving a working relationship with the pharmacist, who is a valued colleague. Which action should you take first?", "options": {"A": "Meet the pharmacist, name the overlap, and draft a written working agreement", "B": "Ask the manager to assign all antihypertensive titration to the physicians", "C": "Continue titrating as before while documenting your rationale in every chart", "D": "Report the contradictory advice to the pharmacist's regulatory college for review"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Meet the pharmacist, name the overlap, and draft a written working agreement): the leadership response to structural ambiguity is to convene the affected roles and write down who does what, including how disagreements about a patient get settled. Handing titration to physicians wastes two competent prescribers and does not address the unnamed ambiguity. Careful documentation records the contradiction without preventing it. A college report is disproportionate when the pharmacist has not practised outside scope.", "case_id": "C062", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner at a family health team in a mid sized Ontario city.\nOver three months, several patients with hypertension have received conflicting dose advice from you and from the team pharmacist, who also adjusts antihypertensives.\nTwo patients stopped their medication because of the confusion.\nStaff describe the situation as a personality clash between the two of you.\nBoth of you are working within your legislated scope, and neither has raised the issue directly with the other.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 784, "domain": "leader", "question": "Three months after the working agreement is signed, the executive director asks how you will know whether it is holding, since similar agreements at other sites faded within a year. You want a method that catches drift early without consuming clinic time. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Circulate the agreement by email and act only if complaints reappear", "B": "Hold a standing monthly meeting with the pharmacist regardless of need", "C": "Track reports of conflicting advice and review the agreement on a set date", "D": "Ask every patient at each visit whether they received contradictory dosing advice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Track reports of conflicting advice and review the agreement on a set date): a partnership that works has an agreed indicator and a scheduled review, which is what catches quiet reversion before patients are affected. Circulating a document and waiting for complaints relies on the failure occurring first. A standing monthly meeting consumes time whether or not anything needs discussing. Asking every patient at every visit is burdensome and captures only those who noticed the contradiction.", "case_id": "C062", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner at a family health team in a mid sized Ontario city.\nOver three months, several patients with hypertension have received conflicting dose advice from you and from the team pharmacist, who also adjusts antihypertensives.\nTwo patients stopped their medication because of the confusion.\nStaff describe the situation as a personality clash between the two of you.\nBoth of you are working within your legislated scope, and neither has raised the issue directly with the other.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 785, "domain": "leader", "question": "The next scheduled benzodiazepine dose is due in fifteen minutes and the medication administration record still shows it as active. The charge nurse asks you what she should do about it. Considering the patient's current respiratory status, which action takes priority right now?", "options": {"A": "Page the physician and request his rationale before any further action", "B": "Hold the dose, assess the patient fully and escalate for urgent review", "C": "File an incident report describing the pattern of prescribing you observed", "D": "Chart your concern and reassess the patient at the next scheduled round"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the dose, assess the patient fully and escalate for urgent review): when the risk is immediate the patient is protected first, which is exactly what a stop the line policy authorizes any team member to do. Waiting for the prescriber's rationale allows a sedating dose to reach an already hypoventilating patient. An incident report is necessary later but documents harm rather than preventing it. Reassessing at the next round accepts an unmonitored interval this patient cannot safely tolerate.", "case_id": "C063", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner on the medical unit of a small rural hospital.\nOn three shifts this month you have watched a locum physician prescribe a benzodiazepine to an eighty one year old woman already receiving hydromorphone, each time without a documented reason.\nTonight she is difficult to rouse, with a respiratory rate of eight and oxygen saturation of eighty nine percent.\nThe physician is off site.\nThe hospital has a stop the line policy and a just culture framework.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 786, "domain": "leader", "question": "The patient is stabilized and transferred for monitoring. Reviewing the events, you find the physician was unaware that this combination carries a respiratory depression risk in frail older adults, and believed brief documentation was optional. Under the hospital's just culture framework, which response to his conduct fits best?", "options": {"A": "Discipline him now, since sedating a frail older adult is reckless", "B": "Console him, since the event arose from an unavoidable system slip", "C": "Coach him and remove the incentive, since this is at risk behaviour", "D": "Take no individual action, since just culture addresses systems only"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Coach him and remove the incentive, since this is at risk behaviour): a shortcut taken without recognizing the risk it creates is met with coaching and a change to whatever made the shortcut attractive. Discipline is reserved for conscious disregard of a substantial known risk, which does not describe a prescriber unaware of the interaction. Consoling fits a slip or lapse by someone who intended the correct action. Just culture addresses both systems and individual behaviour, so doing nothing misapplies it.", "case_id": "C063", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner on the medical unit of a small rural hospital.\nOn three shifts this month you have watched a locum physician prescribe a benzodiazepine to an eighty one year old woman already receiving hydromorphone, each time without a documented reason.\nTonight she is difficult to rouse, with a respiratory rate of eight and oxygen saturation of eighty nine percent.\nThe physician is off site.\nThe hospital has a stop the line policy and a just culture framework.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 787, "domain": "leader", "question": "Two days later you speak with the physician privately, name the specific prescribing events and the specific risk, and he tells you that you are overreacting and that he has practised this way for twenty years. Nothing about his prescribing changes. Which action should you take next?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the concern to him more forcefully during his next scheduled shift", "B": "Notify the regulatory college now, since the internal channel has failed", "C": "Ask colleagues whether they have noticed the same pattern before acting", "D": "Report the specific events and dates in writing to the medical lead"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the specific events and dates in writing to the medical lead): escalation moves to the next level once the direct conversation has failed, and it is made in writing with facts and dates rather than characterizations. Restating the concern more forcefully repeats a step already tried without effect. A college report is premature while an internal channel remains untested. Collecting colleagues' impressions delays action and converts documented events into hearsay.", "case_id": "C063", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner on the medical unit of a small rural hospital.\nOn three shifts this month you have watched a locum physician prescribe a benzodiazepine to an eighty one year old woman already receiving hydromorphone, each time without a documented reason.\nTonight she is difficult to rouse, with a respiratory rate of eight and oxygen saturation of eighty nine percent.\nThe physician is off site.\nThe hospital has a stop the line policy and a just culture framework.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 788, "domain": "leader", "question": "Six weeks pass. You have received no response from the medical lead, and the locum has prescribed the same combination twice more without documentation. You have kept dated records of every event and every report you made. Which action is now most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Accept that the organization has considered the matter and let it rest", "B": "Escalate in writing to the next authority and consider a college report", "C": "Quietly advise nurses not to carry out this physician's sedative orders", "D": "Raise the concern verbally with the chief of staff at an informal opportunity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Escalate in writing to the next authority and consider a college report): a duty to protect patients is not discharged by one report to a level that has not responded, and nursing practice standards across the provinces expect a nurse to keep escalating and, where internal channels have failed and patients remain at risk, to report to the colleague's regulatory college, while statutory mandatory reporting duties are narrower and fall mainly on employers and facility operators. Treating silence as a considered decision leaves a documented risk in place. Advising nurses informally to ignore orders creates a covert workaround without addressing the prescribing. Another verbal conversation repeats the approach that has already failed.", "case_id": "C063", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner on the medical unit of a small rural hospital.\nOn three shifts this month you have watched a locum physician prescribe a benzodiazepine to an eighty one year old woman already receiving hydromorphone, each time without a documented reason.\nTonight she is difficult to rouse, with a respiratory rate of eight and oxygen saturation of eighty nine percent.\nThe physician is off site.\nThe hospital has a stop the line policy and a just culture framework.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 793, "domain": "leader", "question": "A norovirus outbreak closes two wings of a long term care home where you provide primary care. Compliance with the new gowning and hand hygiene protocol must be immediate, uniform and verifiable, and variation is the main risk. As the clinical lead on site this week, which leadership approach fits best?", "options": {"A": "Set explicit expectations, audit compliance daily and address gaps immediately", "B": "Build a shared vision of infection prevention through individual coaching sessions", "C": "Ask each staff member what obstacles get in the way of gowning", "D": "Delegate protocol decisions to whichever staff member is most experienced"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Set explicit expectations, audit compliance daily and address gaps immediately): clear expectations, monitoring and consequences suit routine, high volume, safety critical work during an outbreak, where variation is the hazard. Vision and inspiration change culture over months and are far too slow for a closed unit today. Asking about obstacles supports a demoralized team but does not produce uniform practice this week. Delegating the decision to the most experienced staff member abandons the standardization the situation requires."}, {"id": 801, "domain": "leader", "question": "At a team debrief you realize that a delayed referral you personally missed contributed to a patient's late diagnosis. Two junior staff are present and no one else has noticed your part in it. You want a team that reports its own errors. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Keep the debrief focused on system factors without identifying individual actions", "B": "Raise your own contribution privately with the manager after the debrief ends", "C": "Wait to see whether anyone identifies the referral delay independently first", "D": "Name your own missed referral openly and ask how the process failed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Name your own missed referral openly and ask how the process failed): admitting your own mistakes is the strongest signal that speaking up is safe, and pairing it with a process question keeps the debrief from becoming about blame. Discussing system factors while concealing your own role models exactly the silence you are trying to prevent. Taking it privately to the manager keeps the learning away from the team that needs it. Waiting for someone else to notice makes disclosure conditional on being caught."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "leader", "question": "You walk into a treatment room as a colleague begins injecting a joint on the left knee, while the consent form, the imaging and the patient all identify the right knee. He is mid procedure and has not paused for a checklist. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Say stop immediately and state that the consent identifies the other knee", "B": "Wait until the injection finishes, then raise the discrepancy with him privately", "C": "Leave the room and inform the clinical lead of the discrepancy at once", "D": "Ask the patient afterward which knee has been troubling him most recently"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Say stop immediately and state that the consent identifies the other knee): when the risk is immediate the patient is protected first, and a stop the line moment authorizes any team member to halt a procedure over a safety concern. Waiting for the injection to finish allows the wrong site procedure to be completed. Leaving to find the clinical lead removes the only person present who can prevent it. Questioning the patient afterward gathers information after the harm has occurred."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "leader", "question": "Your clinic has no forum for discussing near misses, and staff say events are mentioned in the corridor and forgotten. You have no formal quality role and no budget. The manager is supportive but busy. Which first step is most likely to start a durable improvement culture?", "options": {"A": "Ask the health authority to assign a quality improvement specialist to the clinic", "B": "Propose a formal quality committee with terms of reference and minutes", "C": "Circulate an anonymous survey asking staff to describe recent near misses", "D": "Add a brief standing safety item to the existing weekly team huddle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add a brief standing safety item to the existing weekly team huddle): building improvement into a meeting that already happens costs nothing, needs no authority and creates the repeated low stakes forum where near misses become discussable. Requesting an external specialist places the work outside the team and outside your control. An anonymous survey collects a snapshot without creating any ongoing route for raising concerns. A formal committee with terms of reference is heavier than a small clinic will sustain and is easily deferred."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "leader", "question": "A student nurse tells you she is worried about a procedure in progress but says she is not senior enough to interrupt. You show her the policy excerpt shown. Based on what the policy actually states, which response to her is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "She should report the concern to her preceptor after the procedure ends", "B": "She may call a safety stop herself and the procedure must pause", "C": "She should record the concern and let the quality committee review it", "D": "She may interrupt only if her preceptor agrees the concern is valid"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She may call a safety stop herself and the procedure must pause): the policy extends the authority to any member of the care team including students, requires the procedure to pause, and states that no supervisory approval is needed. Waiting to tell a preceptor afterward defeats a mechanism designed to work in the moment. Requiring preceptor agreement inserts a permission step the policy explicitly removes. Routing the concern to a committee handles it long after the procedure has finished.", "image": "question_images/q_814.png"}, {"id": 830, "domain": "leader", "question": "You review the summary shown from the launch meeting of a new partnership between your clinic and a community mental health agency. Six months later, referrals are being lost and each side believes the other is tracking them. Which omission best explains the failure?", "options": {"A": "The partnership did not specify a secure electronic method for sending referrals", "B": "No named contact on each side and no scheduled review were agreed", "C": "The agreed number of monthly counselling spaces was set far too low", "D": "The two organizations did not agree on a shared goal at launch"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No named contact on each side and no scheduled review were agreed): partnerships that work name a contact on each side, state what each party contributes, and set a date to review, and the summary records neither the contacts nor the review. A secure fax pathway was explicitly agreed and is workable, so the transmission method is not the gap. The volume of spaces would limit capacity but would not make referrals disappear. A shared goal was recorded, so the failure lies in the operating detail.", "image": "question_images/q_830.png"}, {"id": 839, "domain": "leader", "question": "A dietitian and a physiotherapist on your team have stopped speaking after a disagreement about a patient's activity plan, and each now documents without reading the other's notes. The manager suggests waiting for it to settle. Which action should you take?", "options": {"A": "Wait as suggested, since interprofessional friction usually resolves without intervention", "B": "Convene both to work through the disagreement using an agreed resolution process", "C": "Assign the patient to one of them and remove the other from care", "D": "Report the breakdown to the manager as a professionalism concern about both"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Convene both to work through the disagreement using an agreed resolution process): conflict resolution is a core collaborative competency rather than an unfortunate necessity, and an unresolved rift that has already degraded documentation will not repair itself. Waiting leaves a patient's plan split between two professionals who are not reading each other. Removing one profession from the patient's care sacrifices needed expertise to avoid a conversation. Escalating it as a professionalism complaint punishes the disagreement rather than resolving it."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "educator", "question": "The health authority asks you to distribute the pamphlets this month and report the number handed out. You believe the material will change very little. What is the most appropriate first action before any education about child dental health is delivered here?", "options": {"A": "Rewrite the pamphlet at a grade six reading level and then distribute it", "B": "Distribute the pamphlets as asked and document the barriers you observed", "C": "Survey parents about brushing knowledge using a questionnaire at the nursing station", "D": "Bring the issue to the health committee and ask them to set priorities"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bring the issue to the health committee and ask them to set priorities): community led education, where the community defines the problem and the format, consistently outperforms programs designed elsewhere and shipped in. The committee includes Elders and a community health representative who know what families face at the one store. Rewriting the reading level improves a resource nobody asked for. Distributing pamphlets to generate a count measures paper, not reach, and a clinician designed questionnaire is still an outside agenda entering the community first.", "case_id": "C070", "case_text": "You work at a nursing station in a fly in First Nation community of about 900 people.\nRates of untreated dental decay in preschool children are high, and the regional health authority has sent 400 glossy pamphlets on brushing, printed in English, showing an urban family beside a grocery shelf of fresh produce.\nThe community has one store, intermittent satellite internet, and an active health committee that includes two Elders and a community health representative.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 864, "domain": "educator", "question": "The health committee agrees that early childhood tooth decay matters and suggests reaching families at the community feast and through the school. They ask what format you recommend. Satellite internet drops out most evenings. Which delivery plan best fits this setting and this content?", "options": {"A": "Short in person demonstrations at the feast plus printed one page reminders", "B": "A streaming video series that families can watch at home each week", "C": "A text message reminder program run through the regional health authority", "D": "A password protected online portal holding a brushing log for each child"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short in person demonstrations at the feast plus printed one page reminders): any plan that depends on continuous connectivity fails where broadband is intermittent, so design for offline use and for the gathering places the community itself named. Demonstration also suits a manual skill better than any text does. Streaming video and an online logging portal both collapse when the satellite link drops in the evening. Text reminders assume reliable cellular service and deliver prompts without ever teaching the technique.", "case_id": "C070", "case_text": "You work at a nursing station in a fly in First Nation community of about 900 people.\nRates of untreated dental decay in preschool children are high, and the regional health authority has sent 400 glossy pamphlets on brushing, printed in English, showing an urban family beside a grocery shelf of fresh produce.\nThe community has one store, intermittent satellite internet, and an active health committee that includes two Elders and a community health representative.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 865, "domain": "educator", "question": "An Elder reviews the pamphlet and says the food pictures show items the store does not carry, and that the family shown does not look like anyone here. Which change to the resource matters most for whether families will actually use it?", "options": {"A": "Translate the existing pamphlet text into the language spoken locally", "B": "Reduce the reading level of the text and enlarge the printed font", "C": "Rebuild the content with community members using local foods and images", "D": "Add a section explaining why the pictured foods are unavailable here"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rebuild the content with community members using local foods and images): cultural and linguistic appropriateness has to be designed in from the start with the target group, not patched onto material built elsewhere. Food examples that reflect what is actually on the shelf, and images of people learners recognize, are what make a resource credible. Translating or simplifying the same unusable content keeps the mismatch and only changes its wording. Explaining why the pictured foods are absent draws attention to the flaw without giving families anything they can act on.", "case_id": "C070", "case_text": "You work at a nursing station in a fly in First Nation community of about 900 people.\nRates of untreated dental decay in preschool children are high, and the regional health authority has sent 400 glossy pamphlets on brushing, printed in English, showing an urban family beside a grocery shelf of fresh produce.\nThe community has one store, intermittent satellite internet, and an active health committee that includes two Elders and a community health representative.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 866, "domain": "educator", "question": "Six months after the community led program begins, the health authority asks you for evidence of impact. You want to report something stronger than reaction data while staying realistic about what a nursing station can actually measure. Which indicator best serves that purpose?", "options": {"A": "Number of families attending the feast sessions who rated them as useful", "B": "Proportion of preschool children with a completed dental screening this year", "C": "Number of brushing kits and pamphlets distributed across the community", "D": "Parent scores on a knowledge quiz given right at the end of each session"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Proportion of preschool children with a completed dental screening this year): a screening rate is a population level uptake measure drawn from nursing station records, and it reflects what families did rather than what they thought or recalled, with the indicator and its release to the authority agreed by the health committee. Attendance combined with a usefulness rating is reaction, the weakest level of evidence. A count of kits and pamphlets measures distribution effort and says nothing about reach or use. An end of session quiz captures short term learning that often fades before any behaviour changes.", "case_id": "C070", "case_text": "You work at a nursing station in a fly in First Nation community of about 900 people.\nRates of untreated dental decay in preschool children are high, and the regional health authority has sent 400 glossy pamphlets on brushing, printed in English, showing an urban family beside a grocery shelf of fresh produce.\nThe community has one store, intermittent satellite internet, and an active health committee that includes two Elders and a community health representative.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 874, "domain": "educator", "question": "A 55 year old man brings a printed American page recommending yearly prostate specific antigen screening for every man his age, and asks why you have never ordered it. He is well, with no family history and no urinary symptoms. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell him the page is unreliable and recommend a Canadian website instead", "B": "Order the test, since he has requested it after reading about the benefits", "C": "Explain that screening decisions in Canada are made by provincial programs", "D": "Note which parts apply here and discuss the Canadian screening recommendation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Note which parts apply here and discuss the Canadian screening recommendation): most online health material a Canadian client finds is American, and screening intervals, drug availability and coverage advice all differ, so the useful move is to separate what still applies from what does not. That respects the effort he put into reading and keeps him bringing material to you. Dismissing the source as unreliable teaches him to stop sharing what he reads. Ordering a test purely on request skips shared decision making, and attributing the decision to provincial programs deflects a conversation he is entitled to have with you."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "educator", "question": "A client with hypertension lists several reasons she does not want to start medication, including her mother's side effects, the monthly cost, and a fear of lifelong dependence. You notice yourself preparing to counter each reason in turn. Which response best avoids the righting reflex?", "options": {"A": "Reflect her concerns back and ask what she sees as her options", "B": "Address each concern with evidence on tolerability and provincial drug coverage", "C": "Agree to defer medication and reassess her blood pressure in six months", "D": "Explain that untreated hypertension carries a higher risk than the side effects"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reflect her concerns back and ask what she sees as her options): the righting reflex is the urge to argue the other side, and it reliably pushes the client into defending the position she started with. Reflective listening followed by an open question lets her voice her own change talk instead. Answering each objection with evidence is exactly the argument that generates counter argument. Conceding and deferring abandons the conversation, and warning that the untreated risk is greater is the same correction delivered as a threat."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "educator", "question": "You practise at a community health centre in New Brunswick. A client asks to receive her diabetes teaching and written material in French. Your clinic's material exists only in English, and a French speaking colleague works on Tuesdays. Which response best reflects your obligations here?", "options": {"A": "Provide the English material and translate the key points verbally today", "B": "Arrange service in French, since the province is officially bilingual", "C": "Offer a family member the English material to translate for her at home", "D": "Refer her to a francophone clinic in the nearest larger urban centre"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arrange service in French, since the province is officially bilingual): New Brunswick is the only officially bilingual province and the obligation to offer services in both official languages is constitutional there, so arranging French service, including on a day when a French speaking colleague works, is the requirement rather than a courtesy. An informal verbal translation by an English speaking clinician is neither accurate nor documented. Asking family to translate abandons the professional interpretation standard. Sending her elsewhere transfers your obligation and adds travel she should not need."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "educator", "question": "You have been asked to deliver a diabetes prevention program at an urban Indigenous community organization. You already hold a provincially produced curriculum ready to run, and the organization's staff have not been consulted about content or format. What is the most appropriate first step?", "options": {"A": "Ask the organization to define the priority and shape the program with you", "B": "Deliver the curriculum now and gather feedback for changes in the next cycle", "C": "Adapt the curriculum's images and food examples before delivering it there", "D": "Complete cultural safety training yourself before you begin delivering the program"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask the organization to define the priority and shape the program with you): community led education, where the community sets the priority and the format, consistently outperforms imported programs, and partnership with community organizations, Elders and community health representatives is the posture the Truth and Reconciliation Calls to Action point toward. Running the curriculum first and collecting feedback afterward makes the community a test audience for someone else's plan. Swapping images and food examples is cosmetic adaptation of an outside agenda. Your own training is worthwhile but does not transfer any decision making to the community."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "educator", "question": "A client with prediabetes asks nothing and appears disengaged when you begin discussing risk reduction. You want to share what the prevention evidence shows without lecturing him about his lifestyle. Which sequence best reflects the elicit provide elicit approach in this conversation?", "options": {"A": "Share the evidence, ask for his reaction, then repeat the key message", "B": "Ask his goals, set a target with him, then review it next visit", "C": "Give written material, ask him to read it, then discuss it later", "D": "Ask what he knows, offer the evidence neutrally, ask what he thinks"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask what he knows, offer the evidence neutrally, ask what he thinks): the technique starts by finding out what the person already believes, offers information without persuasion attached, and then hands interpretation back to them, which keeps the conversation collaborative rather than instructional. The closing question is what converts information into the client's own reasoning. Leading with the evidence and then repeating it is a lecture with a pause in the middle. Goal setting is a later step, and handing over reading material defers the exchange entirely."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "advocate", "question": "She adds that she stopped taking the controller inhaler after that visit because she felt she had no say in the plan. Her asthma control has since worsened. Which conclusion about the previous encounter is best supported by the concept of cultural safety?", "options": {"A": "The encounter was culturally safe because no disrespectful language was documented", "B": "Cultural safety cannot be judged until the clinician completes further formal training", "C": "The client's experience of the encounter determines that it was not culturally safe", "D": "The missed appointments indicate non adherence rather than a problem with care"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The client's experience of the encounter determines that it was not culturally safe): cultural safety is assessed by the person receiving care, not by the provider who delivered it. Unsafe cultural practice is any action that demeans, diminishes or disempowers a person's cultural identity, and feeling spoken down to and stripped of any say meets that description. The absence of rude language speaks to the clinician's intent, which is not the measure. Framing the missed doses as non adherence relocates the problem into the client and leaves the encounter unexamined.", "case_id": "C071", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner at an urban community health centre.\nA Cree woman aged 34 attends for follow up of poorly controlled asthma. She missed her last two appointments. At today's visit she is quiet and gives short answers. Near the end she says the previous clinician told her the plan would work if she simply followed instructions, and that she felt spoken down to. Your colleague insists the encounter was respectful because nothing rude was said.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 902, "domain": "advocate", "question": "Your colleague reviews the chart afterward and concludes that because he documented a respectful tone and offered written education, his care met the standard for cultural safety. He asks you to support that view at the team meeting. What does his reasoning most clearly demonstrate?", "options": {"A": "Self certification of cultural safety by the provider, which the concept excludes", "B": "Appropriate use of documentation standards to evaluate the quality of care", "C": "Cultural humility correctly applied to a disputed and difficult clinical interaction", "D": "A reasonable defence, because provider intent determines whether real harm occurred"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Self certification of cultural safety by the provider, which the concept excludes): declaring your own care culturally safe reverses the direction of the assessment, which belongs to the client. Documentation records what the clinician believes happened and cannot substitute for the client's experience of the encounter. Cultural humility would involve examining his own assumptions and the power he held, not defending the interaction. Intent does not neutralize harm, because the standard is the effect on the person receiving care.", "case_id": "C071", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner at an urban community health centre.\nA Cree woman aged 34 attends for follow up of poorly controlled asthma. She missed her last two appointments. At today's visit she is quiet and gives short answers. Near the end she says the previous clinician told her the plan would work if she simply followed instructions, and that she felt spoken down to. Your colleague insists the encounter was respectful because nothing rude was said.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 903, "domain": "advocate", "question": "She has become quiet again and is looking at the door. You have not yet discussed her inhaler technique, her triggers, or the two missed appointments, and the clinic is running behind. Which action is the most appropriate next step in this visit?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her that the previous clinician meant well and move on", "B": "Document non adherence and restate the original asthma action plan", "C": "Refer her to the centre's cultural liaison worker and end the visit early", "D": "Acknowledge what she describes and ask what she wants from today's visit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acknowledge what she describes and ask what she wants from today's visit): naming what she has told you and handing her the agenda begins to redistribute the power in the room. Defending the previous clinician asks her to manage your discomfort and confirms that her account will not be taken seriously. Restating the same plan repeats the behaviour that led her to stop the inhaler. Passing her to a liaison worker outsources a relationship that is yours to repair.", "case_id": "C071", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner at an urban community health centre.\nA Cree woman aged 34 attends for follow up of poorly controlled asthma. She missed her last two appointments. At today's visit she is quiet and gives short answers. Near the end she says the previous clinician told her the plan would work if she simply followed instructions, and that she felt spoken down to. Your colleague insists the encounter was respectful because nothing rude was said.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 904, "domain": "advocate", "question": "Over the following month, four other Indigenous clients tell you they felt dismissed at the same centre, and two have transferred their care elsewhere. You are a staff nurse practitioner, not a manager. Which action is the most appropriate next step for you?", "options": {"A": "Complete an online cultural competency module and record it in your file", "B": "Bring the pattern to the centre as aggregate data and propose changes", "C": "Ask each client individually to submit a written complaint about the clinician", "D": "Reassign all Indigenous clients to the one clinician who has training"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bring the pattern to the centre as aggregate data and propose changes): a recurring barrier described by several clients is an organizational problem, and organizational advocacy is realistic entry level work. Completing a module changes nothing for the clients already leaving and treats a system problem as a personal knowledge gap. Requiring written complaints puts the burden of system change on the people with the least power. Concentrating Indigenous clients with one clinician relieves the rest of the team of any obligation to change.", "case_id": "C071", "case_text": "You are a nurse practitioner at an urban community health centre.\nA Cree woman aged 34 attends for follow up of poorly controlled asthma. She missed her last two appointments. At today's visit she is quiet and gives short answers. Near the end she says the previous clinician told her the plan would work if she simply followed instructions, and that she felt spoken down to. Your colleague insists the encounter was respectful because nothing rude was said.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 927, "domain": "advocate", "question": "Your health authority is drafting its response to the health related Calls to Action of the Truth and Reconciliation Commission. A director asks what the health related calls actually ask for, given that they are recommendations rather than binding law. Which description is accurate?", "options": {"A": "They require provinces to transfer all Indigenous health funding to communities", "B": "They require every clinician to obtain certification in a traditional healing practice", "C": "They call for closing measurable health outcome gaps with annual public reporting", "D": "They apply only to federal institutions and not to provincial health authorities"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They call for closing measurable health outcome gaps with annual public reporting): the health related calls ask that gaps in health outcomes be identified and closed, with progress published, alongside recognizing Indigenous healing practices, increasing and retaining Indigenous health professionals and training all health professionals. Nothing in them requires wholesale transfer of provincial funding. Recognizing the value of Indigenous healing practices means working with Indigenous healers and Elders, not certifying every clinician in a practice that is not theirs. The calls are addressed broadly across governments and institutions, including provincial health systems and schools."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "advocate", "question": "At a team huddle, a colleague makes a generalizing comment that clients from a particular community always exaggerate their symptoms. Two staff laugh and the meeting moves on to the next item. You are a staff nurse practitioner, not the team lead. Which action best supports a culturally safe practice environment?", "options": {"A": "Name the comment as stereotyping at the time and explain the clinical harm", "B": "Say nothing publicly but report the colleague to the regulatory college", "C": "Raise the issue at the next annual staff education day on diversity", "D": "Speak to the two staff who laughed rather than to the colleague"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Name the comment as stereotyping at the time and explain the clinical harm): being the person who names a colleague's stereotyping rather than letting it pass is an explicit element of building an inclusive practice environment. Left unchallenged in front of the team, the comment becomes the shared understanding that shapes how those clients are assessed. Complaining to the college is disproportionate as a first response and does nothing about the team norm today. Deferring to an education day months away, or speaking only to bystanders, leaves the statement standing."}, {"id": 940, "domain": "advocate", "question": "A team debriefs a missed diagnosis that occurred during an unusually busy overnight shift. The clinician involved is well regarded, holds strong egalitarian values, and is distressed by the suggestion that bias played any role. Which statement about bias risk here is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Strong egalitarian values reliably protect against bias in rapid clinical decisions", "B": "Bias is unlikely because the clinician had no prior relationship with the client", "C": "Bias operates mainly in clinicians who hold consciously prejudiced attitudes", "D": "Bias risk rises with time pressure and fatigue, regardless of stated values"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bias risk rises with time pressure and fatigue, regardless of stated values): implicit associations operate outside conscious awareness and often contradict what a person sincerely believes, so the risk is highest on the worst shift rather than in the worst person. Egalitarian values are not protective when decisions are made fast with incomplete data. Absence of a prior relationship removes personal history but leaves group level associations fully intact. Restricting bias to consciously prejudiced clinicians is precisely the misunderstanding that keeps teams from examining their own systems."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "advocate", "question": "A northern health authority struggles to keep the two Indigenous nurses it recruited last year. Exit interviews describe isolation, being expected to speak for all Indigenous people, and no pathway to advancement. Which response best reflects the health related Calls to Action?", "options": {"A": "Address retention conditions rather than only recruiting more Indigenous staff", "B": "Hire non Indigenous staff who have completed cultural competency training instead", "C": "Assign the remaining Indigenous nurse to all Indigenous client encounters", "D": "Increase salaries and accept that turnover in northern posts is unavoidable"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Address retention conditions rather than only recruiting more Indigenous staff): the calls ask for both an increase in Indigenous health professionals and their retention in the communities they serve, and the exit interviews describe exactly the conditions that defeat retention. Replacing them with trained non Indigenous staff abandons the workforce commitment entirely. Funnelling all Indigenous clients to one nurse increases the very burden the interviews identified and excuses everyone else from responsibility. Pay matters, but treating turnover as inevitable accepts the isolation and lack of advancement as fixed."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "advocate", "question": "The receptionist has already told her that the clinic cannot see her without provincial coverage, and she is preparing to leave with both children. You want to give her accurate information about what coverage she may already have. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "The Interim Federal Health Program provides temporary coverage for refugee claimants", "B": "Provincial insurance applies immediately to all refugee claimants on arrival", "C": "No coverage exists at all until the claim is finally determined by the tribunal", "D": "Coverage is available only for infectious disease screening and emergency care"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The Interim Federal Health Program provides temporary coverage for refugee claimants): the program provides limited, temporary coverage to protected persons including resettled refugees and claimants not yet eligible for provincial or territorial insurance. Provincial insurance is exactly what she does not yet have, which is the gap the federal program exists to fill. Waiting for a final determination would leave a pregnant woman and two children without care for months. The coverage extends well beyond screening and emergencies, and depending on category includes supplemental and prescription drug benefits.", "case_id": "C077", "case_text": "A 34 year old refugee claimant who arrived in Canada four months ago attends your clinic with her two children.\nShe is not yet eligible for provincial health insurance. She needs prenatal care, immunization catch up for the children, and a prescription she cannot afford. The receptionist tells her the clinic does not bill any federal program. She speaks Dari.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 960, "domain": "advocate", "question": "Her eldest child is eleven and has been translating for her since arrival. The prenatal history you need to take includes questions about a previous pregnancy loss and about violence experienced during migration. Which action is most appropriate before continuing the assessment?", "options": {"A": "Ask her eldest child to interpret, since he speaks both languages well", "B": "Use an online translation application on your phone for the whole of the visit", "C": "Arrange professional interpretation in Dari before conducting the clinical assessment", "D": "Reschedule to a day when a Dari speaking volunteer is at the clinic"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrange professional interpretation in Dari before conducting the clinical assessment): professional interpretation is the standard, and a child must never be used, least of all for questions about pregnancy loss and violence. Placing an eleven year old in that role exposes him to his mother's trauma and makes honest answers impossible for her. Machine translation on a phone is unreliable for clinical detail and offers no confidentiality or accountability. Rescheduling delays prenatal care and immunizations while making access depend on a volunteer's availability.", "case_id": "C077", "case_text": "A 34 year old refugee claimant who arrived in Canada four months ago attends your clinic with her two children.\nShe is not yet eligible for provincial health insurance. She needs prenatal care, immunization catch up for the children, and a prescription she cannot afford. The receptionist tells her the clinic does not bill any federal program. She speaks Dari.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 961, "domain": "advocate", "question": "You confirm that the clinic is eligible to register as a provider under the federal program but has never done so, and that registration takes only a few days to complete. She is 22 weeks pregnant. Which action is the most appropriate for her today?", "options": {"A": "Advise the client to seek a clinic that already bills the federal program", "B": "Register the clinic to bill the program and see her today regardless", "C": "Charge her privately now and reimburse her once coverage is confirmed", "D": "Provide only urgent care today and defer prenatal care until coverage starts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Register the clinic to bill the program and see her today regardless): the fact that not every provider bills the program is itself a barrier, and removing it is within the clinic's control. Prenatal care at 22 weeks is needed now, so the visit should not wait for administrative steps that take days. Sending her to find another clinic assumes she can identify one, reach it and communicate there. Charging her privately asks a claimant with no income to fund care she is already entitled to receive.", "case_id": "C077", "case_text": "A 34 year old refugee claimant who arrived in Canada four months ago attends your clinic with her two children.\nShe is not yet eligible for provincial health insurance. She needs prenatal care, immunization catch up for the children, and a prescription she cannot afford. The receptionist tells her the clinic does not bill any federal program. She speaks Dari.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 962, "domain": "advocate", "question": "Over the next three months, six more claimant families are turned away at your reception desk before ever reaching a clinician. The refusals are consistent and follow the same script each time. Which action is the most appropriate next step for you as a clinician?", "options": {"A": "Keep a personal list of clinics that accept the federal program for referrals", "B": "Advise future claimants to apply for provincial insurance as soon as possible", "C": "Ask the province to shorten its waiting period for new resident coverage", "D": "Change clinic policy to bill the program and train staff on eligibility"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Change clinic policy to bill the program and train staff on eligibility): seven families refused by the same script is a process failure at the front desk, and fixing the policy and the training stops it for everyone who comes next. Keeping a personal referral list depends on you being present and helps only the families who reach you. Telling claimants to apply sooner does not change eligibility rules and leaves the refusals intact. Asking the province to shorten its waiting period is legitimate policy advocacy but is slow and does not address the barrier your own clinic is creating.", "case_id": "C077", "case_text": "A 34 year old refugee claimant who arrived in Canada four months ago attends your clinic with her two children.\nShe is not yet eligible for provincial health insurance. She needs prenatal care, immunization catch up for the children, and a prescription she cannot afford. The receptionist tells her the clinic does not bill any federal program. She speaks Dari.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 972, "domain": "advocate", "question": "A client cannot obtain a needed medication because a provincial formulary rule excludes it for her indication. A student asks you to explain why an exclusion with no clinical rationale persists year after year. Which explanation best reflects the interdependence of policy, practice and systemic issues?", "options": {"A": "Formulary rules follow national clinical guidelines and change when guidelines change", "B": "Exclusions reflect the drug safety concerns identified by the federal regulator", "C": "The rule follows a funding decision that persists because the population lacks influence", "D": "Exclusions are temporary and resolve once a generic version becomes available"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The rule follows a funding decision that persists because the population lacks influence): the clinical barrier sits on top of a budget choice, and that choice survives because the people affected carry little political weight. Recognizing which lever actually holds the rule in place is what makes advocacy effective rather than merely sincere. Formulary decisions consider cost and budget impact alongside guidelines, so guideline change alone does not move them. Federal regulatory approval concerns safety and efficacy, which is a separate question from whether a province will pay, and generic entry lowers price without guaranteeing that the listing criteria change."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "advocate", "question": "You are paid a fee for each participant you enrol in an industry funded trial. A client of yours is marginally eligible and would likely agree if you recommended it, but a standard therapy would suit her clinical situation better. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Enrol her and disclose the per participant payment during the consent discussion", "B": "Recommend the standard therapy and have another clinician discuss the trial", "C": "Enrol her, since trial participation offers close monitoring at no cost", "D": "Decline all industry funded research to eliminate the possibility of conflict"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recommend the standard therapy and have another clinician discuss the trial): acting in the client's best interest means recommending what suits her, and separating the recruitment conversation from your financial interest removes the conflict rather than announcing it. Enrolling her with a disclosure attached leaves the payment influencing the recommendation she relies on. Framing monitoring as the benefit rationalizes a choice made for the wrong reason when a better treatment exists. Refusing all industry funded research overcorrects and would deprive future clients of access to trials that are properly managed."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "advocate", "question": "You want a provincial prescribing restriction changed because it delays care for your clients, and you have documented every affected case in your practice for a year. Your regulatory college has an open consultation and your professional association is preparing a submission. Which action is most likely to be effective?", "options": {"A": "Publish the restriction on social media and encourage clients to complain", "B": "Write personally to the premier describing each of the cases you have documented", "C": "Work through your professional association's submission to the regulatory consultation", "D": "Prescribe outside the restriction and document the clinical justification carefully"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Work through your professional association's submission to the regulatory consultation): individual nurse practitioners rarely change rules alone, and organized professional advocacy through consultations and briefs is how scope has actually expanded in this country. Your year of documented cases is most powerful as evidence inside that submission. Writing to the premier bypasses the body that is actively deciding the question. Public campaigning may be constrained by employment terms and is unlikely to move a technical consultation, while prescribing outside the restriction risks your registration and helps no one for long."}, {"id": 986, "domain": "advocate", "question": "A client with a history of opioid use is refused admission to a residential treatment program because of your region's abstinence before entry rule. She is angry and agrees that you should record exactly what happened. Which action best serves both her care and system change?", "options": {"A": "Record only the clinical findings so as to keep the chart free of opinion", "B": "Write to the program director expressing your personal disagreement with the rule", "C": "Advise her to seek a program in another region that has no such rule", "D": "Document the refusal and its clinical consequences so the pattern becomes visible"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Document the refusal and its clinical consequences so the pattern becomes visible): documenting a refusal of care makes it countable, and aggregated refusals are the evidence that changes an eligibility rule. She has consented to this and it also protects her by recording why treatment did not start. Omitting the refusal to keep the chart neutral erases the only record that the barrier exists. Writing a personal letter of disagreement carries no data and is easily set aside, and sending her to another region assumes she can travel while leaving the local rule untouched."}];
